window.MM = window.MM || {};
window.MM['rs_grand_mock_2'] = [
  {q:"The sciatic nerve exits the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen below the piriformis muscle. Which nerve also exits through this foramen ABOVE piriformis?",a:"Superior gluteal nerve",b:"Pudendal nerve",c:"Inferior gluteal nerve",d:"Nerve to obturator internus",ans:"A",e:"The superior gluteal nerve (L4-L5-S1) exits above piriformis and supplies gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and tensor fascia lata - its injury causes a Trendelenburg gait.<br><br>Take Notes: Above piriformis → superior gluteal nerve and vessels only | Below piriformis → sciatic, inferior gluteal, pudendal, posterior femoral cutaneous, nerve to quadratus femoris | Trendelenburg gait → superior gluteal nerve palsy",t:180},
  {q:"The nerve most commonly injured in anterior dislocation of the shoulder is:",a:"Axillary nerve",b:"Radial nerve",c:"Musculocutaneous nerve",d:"Median nerve",ans:"A",e:"Anterior shoulder dislocation stretches the axillary nerve (C5-C6) as it winds around the surgical neck of the humerus - causing deltoid paralysis and loss of sensation over the regimental badge area (lateral deltoid).<br><br>Take Notes: Axillary nerve → deltoid + teres minor + regimental badge sensation | Test → abduction 0-15° (deltoid) and lateral arm sensation | Also injured in proximal humerus fractures | Posterior dislocation → rare, associated with seizures/electrocution",t:180},
  {q:"The left recurrent laryngeal nerve loops around:",a:"Ligamentum arteriosum (arch of aorta)",b:"Left subclavian artery",c:"Left common carotid artery",d:"Left internal jugular vein",ans:"A",e:"The left RLN loops under the arch of the aorta at the level of the ligamentum arteriosum before ascending in the tracheo-oesophageal groove, making it vulnerable in aortic arch surgery and left mediastinal pathology.<br><br>Take Notes: Left RLN → loops around aortic arch at ligamentum arteriosum | Right RLN → loops around right subclavian artery | Pancoast tumour at left apex → left RLN palsy | Aortic arch aneurysm → hoarseness from left RLN stretch",t:180},
  {q:"Which tarsal bone articulates with the tibia and fibula proximally AND with the calcaneus, navicular, and cuboid distally?",a:"Talus",b:"Navicular",c:"Cuboid",d:"Calcaneus",ans:"A",e:"The talus sits between the leg bones superiorly (forming the ankle mortise with tibia and fibula) and transmits body weight to the calcaneus posteriorly, and via the navicular and cuboid to the forefoot - it has no muscular attachments.<br><br>Take Notes: Talus → no muscular attachments (only ligamentous) | Highest risk of AVN after fracture | Subtalar joint → talus on calcaneus | Blood supply → tenuous (posterior tibial artery branches) | Ankle mortise → tibia + fibula + talus",t:180},
  {q:"The middle meningeal artery, injury to which causes extradural haematoma, is a branch of:",a:"External carotid artery via maxillary artery",b:"Internal carotid artery",c:"Vertebral artery",d:"Middle cerebral artery",ans:"A",e:"The middle meningeal artery arises from the maxillary artery (terminal branch of external carotid), enters the skull through the foramen spinosum, and runs in the epidural space in grooves on the inner surface of the temporal bone.<br><br>Take Notes: Foramen spinosum → middle meningeal artery and meningeal branch of mandibular nerve | Temporal bone fracture → tears MMA → EDH | Lucid interval → classic presentation of EDH | Biconvex hyperdense on CT | Craniotomy required for evacuation",t:180},
  {q:"The nutrient artery to the femur enters its shaft:",a:"Posteriorly in the middle third through the linea aspera",b:"Anteriorly in the upper third",c:"Medially in the lower third",d:"Laterally at the level of the greater trochanter",ans:"A",e:"The nutrient artery to the femur (from the second perforating branch of the profunda femoris) enters posteriorly through the nutrient foramen in the middle third of the femoral shaft near the linea aspera, directing distally.<br><br>Take Notes: Tibial nutrient artery → largest in the body, from posterior tibial artery, enters upper third posteriorly directing distally | Nutrient foramina → entry of nutrient arteries | Fractures proximal to nutrient foramen → avascular distal fragment at higher AVN risk | Femur nutrient foramen → faces distally",t:180},
  {q:"Damage to the ulnar nerve at Guyon's canal at the wrist spares which intrinsic muscle?",a:"Hypothenar muscles",b:"Medial two lumbricals",c:"Adductor pollicis",d:"First dorsal interosseous",ans:"D",e:"At Guyon's canal, the deep branch of the ulnar nerve supplies hypothenar muscles, all interossei, medial two lumbricals, and adductor pollicis - the first dorsal interosseous is supplied by the deep branch which exits distal to Guyon's canal and may be spared depending on the level of compression.<br><br>Take Notes: Guyon canal → medial wrist between pisiform and hook of hamate | Superficial branch → sensation (medial 1.5 fingers) | Deep branch → motor (all ulnar intrinsics) | Carpal tunnel → median nerve (lateral to ulnar) | Cyclist's palsy → Guyon canal compression",t:180},
  {q:"Which muscle is NOT supplied by the femoral nerve?",a:"Sartorius",b:"Pectineus",c:"Iliacus",d:"Short head of biceps femoris",ans:"D",e:"Short head of biceps femoris is supplied by the common peroneal division of the sciatic nerve - all other thigh flexors and knee extensors are femoral nerve territory. Long head of biceps femoris is supplied by the tibial division.<br><br>Take Notes: Femoral nerve → SAIL muscles: Sartorius, pectineus, Articularis genu, Iliacus (+ quadriceps femoris) | Adductor longus/brevis/magnus → obturator nerve | Biceps femoris short head → common peroneal (sciatic) | Biceps femoris long head → tibial (sciatic)",t:180},
  {q:"The transpyloric plane (L1) does NOT pass through:",a:"First lumbar vertebral body",b:"Tip of the 9th costal cartilage",c:"Fundus of the gallbladder",d:"Hilum of the spleen",ans:"D",e:"The hilum of the spleen lies at the level of T10-T12 (upper abdomen, left hypochondrium) - it is NOT at the transpyloric plane (L1). The spleen is too high. The transpyloric plane passes through the pylorus, fundus of GB, hila of kidneys, neck of pancreas, SMA origin, and tips of 9th costal cartilages.<br><br>Take Notes: L1 transpyloric plane → pylorus, fundus GB, both renal hila, pancreatic neck, SMA origin, 9th costal cartilage tip | L4 → aortic bifurcation, iliac crest | T12 → aortic hiatus | Spleen → T9-T11 level (NOT transpyloric)",t:180},
  {q:"An injury at the posterior triangle of the neck during lymph node biopsy results in the patient being unable to abduct their arm above 90 degrees and having a drooping shoulder. The nerve injured is:",a:"Accessory nerve (CN XI)",b:"Long thoracic nerve",c:"Suprascapular nerve",d:"Axillary nerve",ans:"A",e:"The accessory nerve (CN XI) runs superficially through the posterior triangle supplying trapezius - its injury causes shoulder drop, inability to shrug, and loss of shoulder abduction above 90° (the last phase of abduction depends on trapezius rotating the scapula).<br><br>Take Notes: Posterior triangle → bounded by SCM, trapezius, clavicle | CN XI most commonly injured nerve in posterior triangle (iatrogenic during lymph node biopsy) | Trapezius palsy → winging of the scapula (lateral) + shoulder drop | Distinguished from serratus anterior winging (medial border prominent on pushing against wall)",t:180},
  {q:"The flexor digitorum superficialis (FDS) has a unique anatomical arrangement at the wrist because:",a:"Its tendons bifurcate and pass around the profundus tendons before inserting into the middle phalanges",b:"It passes superficial to the flexor retinaculum",c:"It inserts into the distal phalanges",d:"It is supplied by the ulnar nerve",ans:"A",e:"FDS tendons split at the level of the proximal phalanges to allow FDP tendons to pass through (Camper's chiasm), before inserting into the sides of the middle phalanges, allowing independent PIP joint flexion.<br><br>Take Notes: FDS → inserts into middle phalanx, flexes PIP | FDP → inserts into distal phalanx, flexes DIP | Camper's chiasm → FDS splits to allow FDP through | FDS test → hold other fingers extended (isolates FDS) | Both in carpal tunnel with median nerve",t:180},
  {q:"The blood supply to the head of the femur in adults is primarily from:",a:"Medial circumflex femoral artery (MCFA) via retinacular vessels",b:"Anterior circumflex femoral artery",c:"Obturator artery via ligamentum teres",d:"Lateral circumflex femoral artery",ans:"A",e:"In adults, the medial circumflex femoral artery (from the profunda femoris) provides the dominant blood supply via superior retinacular vessels ascending along the femoral neck under the synovium - explaining AVN risk after femoral neck fractures that disrupt these vessels.<br><br>Take Notes: Adults → MCFA retinacular vessels dominant | Children → obturator artery via ligamentum teres significant | MCFA → runs posterior to iliopsoas, then posterior between piriformis and obturator externus | Superior retinacular vessels → most vulnerable in displaced femoral neck fractures | AVN → 15-30% after Garden III/IV",t:180},
  {q:"A fracture of the surgical neck of the humerus most commonly injures which neurovascular structure?",a:"Axillary nerve and posterior circumflex humeral artery",b:"Radial nerve and profunda brachii artery",c:"Musculocutaneous nerve and anterior circumflex artery",d:"Brachial artery and median nerve",ans:"A",e:"The axillary nerve and posterior circumflex humeral artery wind around the surgical neck of the humerus in the quadrangular space - they are the structures most vulnerable in surgical neck fractures and anterior shoulder dislocations.<br><br>Take Notes: Surgical neck → axillary nerve + posterior circumflex humeral artery | Spiral groove (mid-shaft) → radial nerve + profunda brachii | Medial epicondyle → ulnar nerve | Supracondylar → median nerve/brachial artery in children",t:180},
  {q:"The fascia of Denonvilliers separates:",a:"Rectum from the prostate/seminal vesicles anteriorly",b:"Bladder from the rectum",c:"Prostate from the urethra",d:"Sigmoid colon from the left ureter",ans:"A",e:"Denonvilliers fascia (rectovesical fascia) lies between the posterior surface of the prostate and seminal vesicles anteriorly and the rectum posteriorly - it is a key surgical plane in radical prostatectomy and low anterior resection, and injury risks leaving tumour behind or damaging the rectum.<br><br>Take Notes: Denonvilliers fascia → between prostate/seminal vesicles and rectum | In radical prostatectomy → preserved to protect rectum | In rectal cancer surgery → TME dissects in holy plane (behind Denonvilliers, in front of mesorectal fascia) | Waldeyer's fascia → posterior pre-sacral fascia (superior to Denonvilliers)",t:180},
  {q:"The posterior communicating artery (PComA) aneurysm classically presents with complete third nerve palsy including:",a:"Ipsilateral fixed dilated pupil with ptosis and 'down and out' eye position",b:"Medial deviation of the eye (medial rectus intact)",c:"Bilateral ptosis",d:"Contralateral eye deviation",ans:"A",e:"PComA aneurysm compresses CN III at its origin - the peripherally located parasympathetic pupillomotor fibres are compressed first causing mydriasis; the nerve trunk compression causes ptosis (superior tarsal muscle) and down-and-out deviation (superior oblique and lateral rectus continue).<br><br>Take Notes: Down and out → because only SO (CN IV) and LR (CN VI) are functional | Fixed dilated pupil → compressive CN III palsy (surgical emergency) | Medical CN III palsy → ischaemic, pupil-sparing (diabetic third nerve palsy) | Distinguish → painful + pupil involved = compressive | Painless + pupil-sparing = ischaemic",t:180},
  {q:"The internal pudendal artery (branch of internal iliac) is the main blood supply to the perineum. It reaches the perineum by passing through:",a:"Greater sciatic foramen then curves around the ischial spine/sacrospinous ligament to re-enter through lesser sciatic foramen",b:"Lesser sciatic foramen",c:"Greater sciatic foramen below piriformis",d:"Obturator foramen",ans:"A",e:"The internal pudendal artery exits the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen below piriformis, winds around the ischial spine/sacrospinous ligament, then re-enters through the lesser sciatic foramen to enter the perineum in the pudendal (Alcock's) canal.<br><br>Take Notes: Internal pudendal → exits greater sciatic foramen, wraps around ischial spine, enters lesser sciatic foramen → runs in Alcock's canal on lateral wall of ischiorectal fossa | Pudendal nerve block → at ischial spine | Alcock's canal → pudendal nerve, internal pudendal artery and vein",t:180},
  {q:"Which structure passes through the foramen ovale of the skull base?",a:"Mandibular nerve (V3) and accessory meningeal artery",b:"Facial nerve",c:"Middle meningeal artery",d:"Abducens nerve",ans:"A",e:"The foramen ovale transmits the mandibular nerve (V3), the lesser petrosal nerve, the accessory meningeal artery, and an emissary vein - V3 is the only trigeminal division that carries motor fibres (to muscles of mastication).<br><br>Take Notes: Foramen ovale → V3 (mandibular) + lesser petrosal nerve + accessory meningeal artery | Foramen rotundum → V2 (maxillary) | Superior orbital fissure → CN III, IV, V1, VI + superior ophthalmic vein | Foramen spinosum → middle meningeal artery | Foramen lacerum → crossed by ICA (nothing truly passes through)",t:180},
  {q:"The lateral femoral cutaneous nerve of the thigh (L2-L3) is PURELY sensory and is compressed at the inguinal ligament near the ASIS causing meralgia paraesthetica. It exits the pelvis:",a:"Under the inguinal ligament medial to the ASIS",b:"Through the obturator foramen",c:"Through the greater sciatic foramen",d:"Above the inguinal ligament lateral to the femoral artery",ans:"A",e:"The lateral femoral cutaneous nerve of the thigh passes under the inguinal ligament approximately 1 cm medial to the ASIS (or sometimes through it) - entrapment here causes meralgia paraesthetica (burning dysaesthesia and numbness over the anterolateral thigh).<br><br>Take Notes: Pure sensory → NO motor loss in meralgia paraesthetica | Risk factors → obesity, tight belts, pregnancy, prolonged sitting | Diagnosis → clinical (burning anterolateral thigh, relieved by hip flexion) | Treatment → weight loss, posture change, local steroid injection, rarely surgical decompression | Distinguish from femoral nerve injury → no quadriceps weakness",t:180},
  {q:"Volkmann's ischaemic contracture following supracondylar fracture of the humerus results from ischaemia to:",a:"Flexor muscles of the forearm (primarily flexor digitorum profundus and flexor pollicis longus)",b:"The intrinsic muscles of the hand from ulnar nerve damage",c:"Extensor muscles of the forearm",d:"Biceps and brachialis",ans:"A",e:"Volkmann's contracture results from compartment syndrome of the forearm following supracondylar fracture - ischaemia of the deep flexor compartment (FDP, FPL) leads to fibrous replacement causing a fixed flexion deformity of the fingers and wrist.<br><br>Take Notes: Supracondylar fracture → brachial artery/anterior interosseous nerve compression | Compartment syndrome → forearm deep flexors (FDP, FPL) | Classical deformity → wrist flexed, fingers flexed at MCP and PIP | 3Ps of CS → Pain on passive stretch, Pallor, Pulselessness | Treatment → immediate fasciotomy",t:180},
  {q:"Which embryological structure gives rise to the pyramidal lobe of the thyroid?",a:"Thyroglossus duct remnant (persistent distal portion)",b:"Anterior endoderm of the pharyngeal floor",c:"Fourth pharyngeal pouch",d:"Neural crest cells",ans:"A",e:"The pyramidal lobe is a remnant of the inferior end of the thyroglossus duct - it projects superiorly from the isthmus (or left lobe) toward the hyoid bone, found in ~50% of individuals, and is often seen in Graves disease (hypertrophied).<br><br>Take Notes: Thyroglossus duct → descends from foramen caecum at base of tongue to thyroid bed | Cyst → along this tract, most common at hyoid level | Lingual thyroid → failure of descent (foramen caecum) | Thyroglossal cyst excision → Sistrunk operation (includes central hyoid body) | Pyramidal lobe → inferior remnant of duct",t:180},
  {q:"The deep inguinal ring is located:",a:"In the transversalis fascia, 1.25 cm above the midpoint of the inguinal ligament",b:"In the aponeurosis of the external oblique muscle",c:"At the pubic tubercle",d:"In the conjoint tendon",ans:"A",e:"The deep (internal) inguinal ring is an opening in the transversalis fascia, located 1.25 cm above the midpoint of the inguinal ligament - it is lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels (medial to deep ring = direct hernia territory).<br><br>Take Notes: Deep ring → in transversalis fascia, lateral to inferior epigastric vessels | Superficial ring → in external oblique aponeurosis, above pubic tubercle | Indirect hernia → enters at deep ring (lateral to inferior epigastric vessels) | Direct hernia → bulges through Hesselbach triangle (medial to inferior epigastric vessels) | Hesselbach triangle → inferior epigastric vessels laterally, lateral rectus border medially, inguinal ligament inferiorly",t:180},
  {q:"In the cubital fossa, the structure most MEDIAL from lateral to medial is:",a:"Biceps tendon",b:"Brachial artery",c:"Lateral cutaneous nerve of forearm",d:"Median nerve",ans:"D",e:"The cubital fossa contents from lateral to medial: lateral cutaneous nerve of forearm (outside fossa), biceps tendon, brachial artery, median nerve (mnemonic: TAN = Tendon, Artery, Nerve) - the radial nerve lies deep and lateral at the lateral border.<br><br>Take Notes: Cubital fossa boundaries → brachioradialis (lateral), pronator teres (medial), line between epicondyles (superior) | Contents lateral to medial → Tendon (biceps), Artery (brachial), Nerve (median) → TAN | Median cubital vein → roof, commonly used for IV access | Pulse → felt medial to biceps tendon | Brachial artery → compressible here for BP measurement",t:180},
  {q:"The nerve supply to the external anal sphincter and external urethral sphincter is from:",a:"Pudendal nerve (S2-S4, somatic)",b:"Parasympathetic fibres S2-S4 (pelvic splanchnic nerves)",c:"Inferior hypogastric plexus",d:"Ilioinguinal nerve",ans:"A",e:"The external anal sphincter and external urethral sphincter are VOLUNTARY muscles supplied by the pudendal nerve (somatic, S2-S4) via the inferior rectal branch and perineal branch respectively - voluntary control of micturition and defaecation depends on intact pudendal nerve function.<br><br>Take Notes: External sphincters → pudendal nerve (somatic, voluntary) | Internal sphincters → autonomic (sympathetic = contraction, parasympathetic = relaxation) | Cauda equina injury → loss of pudendal nerve → faecal/urinary incontinence | Pudendal nerve block → at ischial spine (obstetric/perineal surgery) | S2 → most important root for pudendal function",t:180},
  {q:"The palatine tonsil receives its main blood supply from:",a:"Facial artery (tonsillar branch)",b:"Ascending pharyngeal artery",c:"Lingual artery",d:"Maxillary artery",ans:"A",e:"The tonsillar branch of the facial artery is the principal blood supply to the palatine tonsil - bleeding after tonsillectomy is commonly from this vessel. The tonsil also receives smaller contributions from the ascending palatine, dorsal lingual, and ascending pharyngeal arteries.<br><br>Take Notes: Facial artery → main supply to palatine tonsil | Post-tonsillectomy haemorrhage → primary (within 24h) vs secondary (5-10 days) | Dangerous area → external carotid artery lies just deep and lateral to the tonsil (no muscle between) | Peritonsillar abscess → between tonsil and superior constrictor muscle",t:180},
  {q:"The floor of the inguinal canal is formed by:",a:"Transversalis fascia",b:"External oblique aponeurosis",c:"Conjoint tendon",d:"Inguinal ligament and lacunar ligament medially",ans:"D",e:"The floor of the inguinal canal is formed by the grooved upper surface of the inguinal ligament throughout, with the lacunar ligament medially. This forms the trough in which the spermatic cord/round ligament rests.<br><br>Take Notes: Floor → inguinal ligament + lacunar ligament (medially) | Roof → internal oblique and transversus abdominis arching fibres | Anterior wall → EO aponeurosis (+ internal oblique laterally) | Posterior wall → transversalis fascia (+ conjoint tendon medially) | Lacunar ligament → triangular expansion of medial inguinal ligament, forms medial boundary of femoral ring",t:180},
  {q:"Which muscle is responsible for the 'power grip' and its loss significantly impairs hand function?",a:"Opponens pollicis",b:"Abductor pollicis brevis",c:"Lumbricals",d:"Flexor digitorum profundus",ans:"D",e:"FDP is responsible for terminal phalanx flexion and is the primary power grip muscle - without FDP, grip strength is severely reduced. It is tested by asking the patient to flex the DIP joint while the PIP is held extended.<br><br>Take Notes: FDP → flexes DIP (power grip) | FDS → flexes PIP | Lumbricals → flex MCP, extend IP (interosseous muscles do the same) | FDP in carpal tunnel syndrome → not affected (common misconception) | Zone II injury (no man's land) → most difficult to repair (FDS and FDP tendons in tight fibro-osseous sheath)",t:180},
  {q:"The artery of Adamkiewicz (great radicular artery) supplies the:",a:"Cervical spinal cord",b:"Upper thoracic spinal cord (T1-T6)",c:"Sacral spinal cord",d:"Lower thoracic/upper lumbar spinal cord (typically T9-T12)",ans:"D",e:"The artery of Adamkiewicz (arteria radicularis magna) is the largest anterior segmental medullary artery, usually arising from T9-T12 (left side in ~75%) - it is the dominant blood supply to the lower two-thirds of the spinal cord, and its injury causes anterior spinal artery syndrome.<br><br>Take Notes: Anterior spinal artery syndrome → bilateral motor loss + bilateral pain/temperature loss, with PRESERVED dorsal column (proprioception/vibration) | Risk → aortic surgery, thoracic spine surgery | Arises from T9-T12 (left side more common) | Watershed zone → T4 (end of posterior intercostal supply and before Adamkiewicz) → most vulnerable to ischaemia",t:180},
  {q:"A patient with a penetrating injury to zone II of the neck (between cricoid and angle of mandible) has mandatory surgical exploration EXCEPT when:",a:"Asymptomatic with no hard signs of vascular or aerodigestive injury",b:"Expanding haematoma is present",c:"Active haemorrhage is occurring",d:"Air bubbling from the wound",ans:"A",e:"Zone II mandatory exploration is now challenged by the selective approach - haemodynamically stable patients without hard signs of injury (expanding haematoma, active haemorrhage, airway compromise, neurological deficit, subcutaneous emphysema) may undergo CT angiography instead of mandatory exploration.<br><br>Take Notes: Zone I → below cricoid, high morbidity, may need median sternotomy | Zone II → cricoid to mandible, most common, easiest to explore | Zone III → above mandible, difficult access, angioembolisation preferred | Hard signs → mandatory surgery | Soft signs → CT angiography first | Platysma violation alone → does not mandate exploration",t:180},
  {q:"The radial nerve at the elbow divides into the superficial radial nerve and the posterior interosseous nerve (PIN). A PIN palsy at the lateral epicondyle produces:",a:"Finger drop without wrist drop and without sensory loss",b:"Wrist drop with first dorsal web space sensory loss",c:"Complete wrist and finger drop with sensory loss",d:"Thenar wasting only",ans:"A",e:"PIN is a pure motor nerve - its palsy causes finger drop (extensor digitorum, extensor indicis) but wrist extension is preserved (extensor carpi radialis longus is supplied proximal to the PIN origin and is always spared) and there is NO sensory loss.<br><br>Take Notes: PIN palsy → finger drop, NO wrist drop (ECRL preserved), NO sensory loss | Radial nerve at spiral groove → wrist drop + finger drop + first web space sensory loss | Superficial radial nerve → pure sensory (dorsal first web space) | Causes of PIN palsy → radial head dislocation (Monteggia), lipoma, rheumatoid synovitis at elbow, arcade of Frohse (proximal supinator)",t:180},
  {q:"The duct of Wirsung (main pancreatic duct) and common bile duct typically join:",a:"Outside the duodenal wall",b:"Within the substance of the head of the pancreas",c:"At the level of the first part of the duodenum",d:"Within the wall of the second part of the duodenum to form the hepatopancreatic ampulla (ampulla of Vater)",ans:"D",e:"The main pancreatic duct and CBD join within the duodenal wall (not outside) to form the hepatopancreatic ampulla, controlled by the sphincter of Oddi, opening at the major duodenal papilla on the posteromedial wall of the second part of the duodenum.<br><br>Take Notes: Ampulla of Vater → where CBD + pancreatic duct meet WITHIN duodenal wall | Major duodenal papilla → D2 (posteromedial wall) | Minor papilla → D2 (proximal to major), accessory pancreatic duct (Santorini) | Sphincter of Oddi → controls flow of bile and pancreatic juice | ERCP → accesses ampulla endoscopically via D2",t:180},
  {q:"The coronary sinus drains into the:",a:"Right ventricle",b:"Left atrium",c:"Right atrium between the IVC opening and the tricuspid valve",d:"Superior vena cava",ans:"C",e:"The coronary sinus (the main venous collector of the heart) drains into the right atrium between the opening of the IVC and the atrioventricular orifice (tricuspid valve), guarded by the Thebesian valve (often incomplete).<br><br>Take Notes: Coronary sinus → receives great cardiac vein (from LAD territory), middle and small cardiac veins | Right atrium drainage → SVC + IVC + coronary sinus + Thebesian veins | Coronary sinus access → used for cardiac resynchronisation therapy (CRT) lead placement | CABG targets → LAD, RCA, circumflex territory vessels",t:180},
  {q:"The most common congenital heart defect overall is:",a:"Tetralogy of Fallot",b:"Transposition of great arteries",c:"Ventricular septal defect (VSD)",d:"Atrial septal defect",ans:"C",e:"VSD is the most common congenital heart defect overall (~30% of all CHD), most commonly in the perimembranous region - small ones close spontaneously, large ones cause left-to-right shunt, pulmonary hypertension, and eventual Eisenmenger syndrome if uncorrected.<br><br>Take Notes: VSD → most common CHD overall | ASD → most common CHD diagnosed in adults (often missed in childhood) | TOF → most common cyanotic CHD after infancy | TGA → most common cyanotic CHD presenting at birth | VSD → holosystolic murmur, lower left sternal border, louder = smaller (restrictive VSD)",t:180},
  {q:"The hepatic veins drain into the IVC. How many main hepatic veins are there and where do they drain?",a:"Three hepatic veins (right, middle, left) draining into the IVC at its intrahepatic segment just below the diaphragm",b:"Two hepatic veins draining at the porta hepatis",c:"One main hepatic vein joining the portal vein",d:"Four hepatic veins all draining separately into the right atrium",ans:"A",e:"Three main hepatic veins (right, middle, left) drain into the suprahepatic IVC just below the diaphragm at T8 level - the middle hepatic vein often joins the left hepatic vein before entering the IVC. The caudate lobe drains directly into the IVC via accessory veins.<br><br>Take Notes: Hepatic veins → intersegmental (drain adjacent segments) | Caudate lobe (segment I) → directly into IVC (independent drainage, preserved in Budd-Chiari) | Hepatic vein ligation → during liver resection | Pringle manoeuvre → controls inflow (not hepatic veins, which are outflow) | Budd-Chiari → hepatic vein occlusion, caudate lobe spared",t:180},
  {q:"The sigmoid colon is unique because it has:",a:"A long mesentery (sigmoid mesocolon) giving it mobility and predisposing to volvulus",b:"No mesentery",c:"A fixed retroperitoneal position",d:"Three complete taeniae coli merging at the appendix",ans:"A",e:"The sigmoid colon has a long, inverted V-shaped sigmoid mesocolon - this mobility allows volvulus when the mesocolon is long and the base narrow (particularly in elderly, constipated, institutionalised patients).<br><br>Take Notes: Sigmoid volvulus → most common site of colonic volvulus (coffee bean sign on AXR) | Caecal volvulus → second most common | Sigmoid mesocolon → inverted V-shaped base | Attachment at left pelvic brim → apex over left common iliac artery | Long sigmoid → predisposes in high-fibre diet populations and institutionalised patients",t:180},
  {q:"The nerve to the subscapularis muscle is the upper and lower subscapular nerves arising from which cord of the brachial plexus?",a:"Posterior cord",b:"Lateral cord",c:"Medial cord",d:"Anterior division",ans:"A",e:"Both upper and lower subscapular nerves arise from the posterior cord (C5-C6) of the brachial plexus. The lower subscapular nerve also supplies teres major - both muscles cause medial rotation and adduction of the humerus.<br><br>Take Notes: Posterior cord branches → upper subscapular (subscapularis), thoracodorsal (latissimus dorsi), lower subscapular (subscapularis + teres major), axillary (deltoid, teres minor), radial nerve | Medial rotation of shoulder → subscapularis + teres major + pectoralis major + latissimus dorsi | Nerve to serratus anterior → long thoracic (C5-7), NOT from cords",t:180},
  {q:"The lymphatics of the testis drain to:",a:"Para-aortic (lumbar) lymph nodes at L1-L2",b:"Inguinal lymph nodes",c:"Iliac lymph nodes",d:"Obturator lymph nodes",ans:"A",e:"The testis develops in the retroperitoneum and descends into the scrotum retaining its original lymphatic drainage to the para-aortic (lumbar) lymph nodes at L1-L2 - this explains why testicular cancer metastasises to para-aortic nodes, NOT inguinal nodes.<br><br>Take Notes: Testis lymphatics → para-aortic (retroperitoneal) nodes at L1-L2 | Scrotal skin lymphatics → inguinal nodes | Orchidectomy → inguinal approach (NOT scrotal biopsy to avoid contaminating inguinal nodes) | Penile lymphatics → inguinal nodes | Retroperitoneal lymph node dissection (RPLND) → for germ cell tumours",t:180},
  {q:"The phrenic nerve (C3-C4-C5) runs on which surface of the anterior scalene muscle?",a:"Posterior surface",b:"Lateral surface",c:"Medial border",d:"Anterior surface",ans:"D",e:"The phrenic nerve descends on the anterior surface of scalenus anterior (under the investing fascia) - it passes from lateral to medial as it descends, allowing it to enter the thorax by crossing the first part of the subclavian artery medially.<br><br>Take Notes: Phrenic nerve → C3, C4, C5 (keeps the diaphragm alive) | Runs on anterior surface of scalenus anterior | Brachial plexus → on posterior surface of scalenus anterior | Cervical rib → may compress subclavian artery and brachial plexus (NOT phrenic nerve) | Phrenic nerve block → used for hiccups, or to paralise hemidiaphragm during surgery",t:180},
  {q:"The articular surface of the lateral femoral condyle is SMALLER than the medial condyle. This anatomical difference explains the terminal rotation of the knee joint called the 'screw home mechanism', which involves:",a:"Medial rotation of the tibia during terminal knee flexion",b:"Posterior glide of the femur during knee flexion only",c:"Anterior glide of the tibia on the femur during extension",d:"Lateral rotation of the tibia during terminal knee extension (or medial rotation of femur on fixed tibia)",ans:"D",e:"The medial femoral condyle is larger than the lateral, so the lateral compartment finishes its arc of motion before the medial - in full extension, the femur medially rotates on the fixed tibia (or tibia laterally rotates), 'locking' the knee into full extension. Popliteus 'unlocks' the knee by medially rotating the tibia.<br><br>Take Notes: Screw-home mechanism → knee locks in full extension (lateral rotation of tibia) | Popliteus → unlocks knee (medial rotation of tibia at initiation of flexion) | Clinical significance → ACL prevents anterior tibial subluxation; PCL prevents posterior subluxation | Menisci → deepen tibial plateaux, more mobile laterally",t:180},
  {q:"Which structure is at risk during thyroid surgery when identifying the external laryngeal nerve (superior laryngeal nerve, external branch)?",a:"Inferior thyroid artery",b:"Recurrent laryngeal nerve",c:"Middle thyroid vein",d:"Superior thyroid artery and its branching near the superior pole of the thyroid",ans:"D",e:"The external laryngeal nerve (ELN) runs close to the superior thyroid artery, descending medially to supply the cricothyroid muscle - during superior pole thyroid surgery, the ELN is at risk when dividing the superior thyroid vessels, causing a subtle but important change in voice pitch.<br><br>Take Notes: ELN → supplies cricothyroid (tensor of vocal cord) | Injury → loss of high-pitched voice, inability to sing high notes (Amelita Galli-Curci sign) | RLN → all other intrinsic laryngeal muscles | ELN injury rate → 0-58% in thyroidectomy (often unrecognised) | Cernea classification → ELN relationship to superior thyroid artery",t:180},
  {q:"The right lung has how many segments compared to the left?",a:"Both have 8 segments",b:"Right has 8, left has 10",c:"Both have 10 segments",d:"Right has 10, left has 8-9 (medial basal segment may be separate or combined)",ans:"D",e:"The right lung has 10 bronchopulmonary segments (3 upper, 2 middle, 5 lower), while the left lung has 8-9 due to fusion - the apical-posterior segment (upper lobe) and anteromedial basal segments (lower lobe) are fused, giving 8 or 9 depending on the source.<br><br>Take Notes: Right lung → 3 lobes, 2 fissures (major and minor/horizontal) | Left lung → 2 lobes, 1 fissure (major) | Right middle lobe → homologous to left lingula | Eparterial bronchus → right upper lobe bronchus (above right pulmonary artery) | All others → hyparterial (below pulmonary artery)",t:180},
  {q:"Hartmann's pouch is the name for the dilatation at the junction of the cystic duct and:",a:"Gallbladder neck",b:"Common hepatic duct",c:"Common bile duct",d:"Gallbladder fundus",ans:"A",e:"Hartmann's pouch is a dilatation at the gallbladder neck-cystic duct junction - a stone impacted here causes Mirizzi syndrome by compressing the common hepatic duct. It is also an important anatomical landmark during cholecystectomy.<br><br>Take Notes: Hartmann's pouch → GB neck dilatation | Stone impacted here → Mirizzi syndrome (extrinsic compression of CHD → jaundice) | Type I Mirizzi → extrinsic compression | Type II → fistula between GB and CHD | Treatment Mirizzi → cholecystectomy ± biliary reconstruction | Not to be confused with Hartmann's operation (sigmoid colectomy)",t:180},
  {q:"The popliteal artery gives off its FIRST branch as:",a:"Anterior tibial artery",b:"Posterior tibial artery",c:"Genicular arteries (superior and inferior genicular arteries)",d:"Sural arteries",ans:"C",e:"The popliteal artery gives off the genicular branches (superior medial and lateral, inferior medial and lateral, and middle genicular) before terminating at the inferior border of popliteus by dividing into anterior and posterior tibial arteries.<br><br>Take Notes: Popliteal artery termination → lower border of popliteus (not knee joint) | First branches → genicular arteries | Divides into → anterior tibial (through interosseous membrane) + posterior tibial (continues down posterior compartment) | Peroneal artery → from posterior tibial | Popliteal aneurysm → most common peripheral artery aneurysm",t:180},
  {q:"The upper border of the thyroid gland is at the level of the:",a:"Hyoid bone",b:"Junction of C5-C6 vertebrae",c:"Cricoid cartilage",d:"Thyroid cartilage oblique line",ans:"D",e:"The thyroid gland's superior poles extend to the level of the oblique line of the thyroid cartilage (approximately C4-C5) - the isthmus lies in front of the 2nd to 4th tracheal rings, and the inferior poles extend to the level of the 5th-6th tracheal rings.<br><br>Take Notes: Thyroid isthmus → covers tracheal rings 2-4 | Cricothyroid membrane → between thyroid and cricoid cartilage, access point for emergency airway | Tracheostomy → below isthmus (2-4 rings) or through isthmus (divide it) | Emergency cricothyroidotomy → below isthmus level | RLN enters larynx at inferior border of inferior constrictor",t:180},
  {q:"The nerve that carries afferent fibres from the carotid body (chemoreceptors) and carotid sinus (baroreceptors) to the brainstem is:",a:"Vagus nerve (X)",b:"Hypoglossal nerve (XII)",c:"Internal carotid nerve (sympathetic)",d:"Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)",ans:"D",e:"CN IX (Hering's nerve, a branch of glossopharyngeal) carries afferent signals from both the carotid body (hypoxia) and carotid sinus (pressure) to the nucleus tractus solitarius in the medulla, where reflex responses are integrated.<br><br>Take Notes: CN IX → carotid body (chemoreceptors) + carotid sinus (baroreceptors) | CN X → aortic body + aortic arch baroreceptors | Carotid sinus hypersensitivity → syncope from excessive baroreceptor response | Carotid endarterectomy → may manipulate carotid sinus → bradycardia | Atropine → blocks vagal response to carotid sinus massage",t:180},
  {q:"In the embryological development of the gut, the intestinal rotation is 270 degrees counterclockwise around the superior mesenteric artery. Malrotation typically results in which position of the duodenojejunal flexure?",a:"Left of the vertebral column, at or below the transpyloric plane",b:"Posterior to the stomach",c:"Normal position left of midline, above the transpyloric plane",d:"Right of the midline and low, predisposing to Ladd's bands and midgut volvulus",ans:"D",e:"In malrotation, the duodenojejunal (DJ) flexure fails to move to its normal position (left of L2) - it remains to the right of midline and low. Ladd's bands form between the caecum and the right posterior abdominal wall, crossing and obstructing the duodenum. Midgut volvulus (around SMA) is the most feared complication.<br><br>Take Notes: Normal DJ flexure → left of midline, level of L1-2, fixed by ligament of Treitz | Malrotation → DJ flexure right of midline, caecum high/central | Ladd's bands → from caecum to right peritoneal wall, cross duodenum | Volvulus → around SMA (whirlpool sign on doppler USS) | Treatment → Ladd's procedure (divide bands, straighten duodenum, appendicectomy, widen mesentery base)",t:180},
  {q:"The Fick principle states that cardiac output can be calculated as:",a:"Heart rate × stroke volume",b:"Stroke volume × ejection fraction",c:"Mean arterial pressure ÷ systemic vascular resistance",d:"Oxygen consumption ÷ (arteriovenous oxygen difference)",ans:"D",e:"CO (Fick) = O2 consumption (mL/min) / (arterial O2 content - venous O2 content). Normal CO = 5 L/min, O2 consumption = 250 mL/min, a-v O2 difference = 50 mL/L, so CO = 250/50 = 5 L/min.<br><br>Take Notes: Fick principle → measures true CO via O2 balance | Gold standard for CO measurement | Thermodilution (Swan-Ganz) → clinical alternative | High CO states → sepsis, thyrotoxicosis, AVM, anaemia | Low CO → cardiogenic shock, cardiac tamponade",t:180},
  {q:"Type IIA muscle fibres (fast oxidative-glycolytic) differ from Type IIB (fast glycolytic) because Type IIA:",a:"Fatigue faster and are used for explosive short bursts only",b:"Have no aerobic capacity",c:"Are only present in cardiac muscle",d:"Have intermediate fatigability, good mitochondrial density, and are used for sustained high-intensity exercise",ans:"D",e:"Type IIA (fast oxidative-glycolytic) fibres have both good oxidative and glycolytic capacity - they are recruited for sustained high-intensity exercise (middle-distance running) and are intermediate in size and fatigability between Type I (slow oxidative) and Type IIB (fast glycolytic).<br><br>Take Notes: Type I → slow twitch, oxidative, fatigue-resistant, postural muscles (soleus) | Type IIA → fast, oxidative + glycolytic, intermediate | Type IIB (IIX) → fast glycolytic, fastest, most fatigable, explosive power | Athletes → endurance training converts IIB to IIA | Denervation → muscle atrophy, type grouping on biopsy",t:180},
  {q:"The law of Laplace as applied to hollow organs states that wall tension = pressure × radius. This means that:",a:"Larger ventricles need less wall tension to generate the same pressure",b:"The law only applies to blood vessels, not cardiac chambers",c:"Tension and radius are inversely proportional",d:"Larger ventricles require MORE wall tension to generate the same pressure (explaining why dilated hearts have higher O2 demand)",ans:"D",e:"T = P × r - for a dilated ventricle (increased radius), much greater wall tension is required to maintain the same ejection pressure, massively increasing myocardial O2 demand and accelerating heart failure progression.<br><br>Take Notes: Laplace law in heart → explains why dilated cardiomyopathy has high O2 demand and poor efficiency | Laplace in alveoli → why small alveoli collapse without surfactant | Laplace in vessels → explains why large aneurysms have higher rupture risk (higher wall tension) | Afterload reduction (ACEi, vasodilators) → reduces wall tension in dilated hearts",t:180},
  {q:"The forced expiratory volume in 1 second (FEV1) in obstructive lung disease is typically:",a:"Normal or increased",b:"Reduced FVC only with normal FEV1",c:"Reduced with FEV1/FVC ratio >70%",d:"Reduced, with FEV1/FVC ratio <70%",ans:"D",e:"Obstructive disease (COPD, asthma, bronchiectasis) reduces FEV1 more than FVC due to airflow limitation (air trapping) - the FEV1/FVC ratio falls below 70% (or below the lower limit of normal). Restrictive disease reduces both proportionally keeping the ratio normal or elevated.<br><br>Take Notes: Obstruction → FEV1 reduced more than FVC → FEV1/FVC <70% | Restriction → both reduced proportionally → FEV1/FVC normal or >70% | TLC increased in obstruction (hyperinflation) | TLC reduced in restriction | Reversibility → if FEV1 increases ≥12% and ≥200mL with bronchodilator → asthma",t:180},
  {q:"Cardiac output is measured by thermodilution via Swan-Ganz catheter. The catheter is placed in the:",a:"Right atrium",b:"Aorta",c:"Left ventricle",d:"Pulmonary artery",ans:"D",e:"The Swan-Ganz (pulmonary artery) catheter is floated through the right heart into the pulmonary artery, where it measures pulmonary artery wedge pressure (PAWP, indirect measure of left atrial pressure), mixed venous O2 saturation, and cardiac output via thermodilution.<br><br>Take Notes: PAWP → reflects left atrial filling pressure (preload of left ventricle) | Normal PAWP → 6-12 mmHg | PAWP elevated → left heart failure, mitral stenosis | PAWP normal in cardiogenic shock from RV failure | Thermodilution CO → cold saline injected, temperature decrease in PA measured → CO calculated",t:180},
  {q:"The jugular venous pulse (JVP) - the A wave represents:",a:"Tricuspid valve opening",b:"Tricuspid valve closure",c:"Ventricular relaxation",d:"Right atrial contraction (occurs just before the carotid pulse)",ans:"D",e:"The A wave in the JVP is produced by right atrial contraction (systole of the atrium) - it occurs just before the carotid pulse. Giant A waves occur when the atrium contracts against a resistant tricuspid valve (TS, pulmonary stenosis, RVH, complete heart block) or AV dissociation (cannon A waves).<br><br>Take Notes: JVP waves → A (atrial contraction) → C (tricuspid valve closure) → X descent (atrial relaxation) → V (venous filling while TV closed) → Y descent (TV opens) | Absent A wave → atrial fibrillation (no coordinated atrial contraction) | Giant A wave → tight TS, PS, RVH | Cannon A waves → CHB, AV dissociation | Kussmaul sign → JVP rises on inspiration (constrictive pericarditis, RV failure, TS)",t:180},
  {q:"In the ABO blood group system, a person with blood group O has:",a:"A and B antigens on red cells, neither anti-A nor anti-B antibodies",b:"A antigens only on red cells",c:"Anti-A antibody only",d:"Neither A nor B antigens on red cells, but has both anti-A and anti-B antibodies in plasma",ans:"D",e:"Group O individuals have neither A nor B antigens on their red cells, but naturally carry both anti-A and anti-B IgM antibodies in their plasma. Group O is the universal donor (red cells), while group AB is the universal plasma donor (no antibodies).<br><br>Take Notes: Blood group antigens → on red cell surface | Antibodies → naturally occurring IgM in plasma | Cross-match → patient's serum + donor's red cells (detects incompatibility) | Type and screen → determines group and screens for irregular antibodies | Universal red cell donor → O negative | Universal plasma donor → AB (no anti-A or anti-B)",t:180},
  {q:"The hepatocyte exports VLDL into the blood. The main lipid component of VLDL is:",a:"Cholesterol esters (primary)",b:"Free fatty acids (primary)",c:"Phospholipids (primary)",d:"Triglycerides (primary)",ans:"D",e:"VLDL is triglyceride-rich (55-65% triglycerides) - the liver assembles VLDL to export excess triglycerides from de novo lipogenesis (insulin-stimulated glucose conversion) and dietary fatty acid overflow to peripheral tissues for storage or oxidation.<br><br>Take Notes: VLDL (endogenous) → liver-derived, TG-rich | Chylomicron (exogenous) → intestinal, dietary TG-rich | VLDL → IDL → LDL (sequential TG removal by LPL and hepatic lipase) | Hypertriglyceridaemia → elevated VLDL (or chylomicrons in Type I) | Fibrates → reduce VLDL by increasing LPL activity",t:180},
  {q:"The oxygen dissociation curve for foetal haemoglobin (HbF) is shifted to the LEFT compared to adult HbF because:",a:"HbF has gamma chains instead of beta chains, which bind 2,3-DPG less avidly",b:"HbF has a higher iron content",c:"Foetal red cells are smaller",d:"HbF has delta chains instead of alpha chains",ans:"A",e:"HbF has gamma (not beta) chains - gamma chains bind 2,3-DPG with much lower affinity than beta chains, resulting in less allosteric inhibition of O2 binding, shifting the ODC leftward (higher O2 affinity). This allows HbF to extract O2 from maternal HbA in the placenta.<br><br>Take Notes: HbF → alpha-2, gamma-2 chains | HbA → alpha-2, beta-2 chains | HbF left shift → better O2 uptake at lower PO2 (placenta) | Sickle cell → HbS (alpha-2, beta-2 S) | Persistence of HbF → protective in sickle cell disease | P50 of HbF → ~19 mmHg (vs ~27 mmHg for HbA)",t:180},
  {q:"During exercise, the increased delivery of O2 to working muscles is achieved by all mechanisms EXCEPT:",a:"Increased cardiac output",b:"Increased arteriolar vasodilation in working muscles",c:"Right shift of oxythaemoglobin dissociation curve",d:"Increased haemoglobin synthesis immediately",ans:"D",e:"Haemoglobin synthesis cannot increase immediately - it requires days to weeks of EPO stimulation. The acute mechanisms are increased CO, local vasodilation (metabolic hyperaemia), right-shifted ODC (Bohr effect from increased CO2, H+, temperature, 2,3-DPG), and increased O2 extraction.<br><br>Take Notes: Immediate exercise responses → increased CO + local vasodilation + right-shifted ODC + increased O2 extraction | Chronic → increased EPO → polycythaemia (altitude adaptation) | Increased VO2 max → best measure of cardiovascular fitness | Fick principle → VO2 = CO × (CaO2 - CvO2)",t:180},
  {q:"The normal wedge pressure (PAWP) reflects filling pressure of which cardiac chamber?",a:"Right ventricle",b:"Pulmonary artery",c:"Right atrium",d:"Left ventricle (indirectly via left atrium)",ans:"D",e:"When the Swan-Ganz catheter balloon is inflated (wedge position), it measures pressure transmitted backward from the pulmonary veins → left atrium → reflecting left ventricular end-diastolic pressure - the best bedside measure of left heart preload.<br><br>Take Notes: PAWP (wedge pressure) → reflects LVEDP | Normal PAWP → 6-12 mmHg | PAWP >18 mmHg → pulmonary oedema threshold | PAWP low in hypovolaemia | PAWP high in LVF, mitral stenosis | CVP → reflects right heart filling | Septic shock → low PAWP + high CO + low SVR",t:180},
  {q:"Renin release from the juxtaglomerular apparatus is inhibited by:",a:"Reduced renal perfusion pressure",b:"Reduced stretch in the afferent arteriole",c:"Beta-1 adrenergic stimulation",d:"Increased NaCl delivery to the macula densa",ans:"D",e:"Increased NaCl delivery to the macula densa cells (signalling adequate volume) inhibits renin release - conversely, reduced NaCl delivery (hypovolaemia, low GFR) stimulates renin release. This is the tubuloglomerular feedback mechanism.<br><br>Take Notes: Renin STIMULATED by → low perfusion pressure + low NaCl at macula densa + sympathetic stimulation (beta-1) | Renin INHIBITED by → high NaCl at macula densa + high perfusion pressure + ANP + angiotensin II (negative feedback) | RAAS → renin → angiotensin I → ACE → angiotensin II → aldosterone | Beta-blockers reduce renin release",t:180},
  {q:"The sinoatrial node is located at the junction of:",a:"The right atrium and the inferior vena cava",b:"The left atrium and the pulmonary veins",c:"The right atrium and the right ventricle",d:"The superior vena cava and the right atrium (sulcus terminalis)",ans:"D",e:"The SA node lies at the junction of the SVC and the right atrium, just beneath the epicardium at the superior end of the crista terminalis (sulcus terminalis externally) - it is supplied primarily by the SA node artery, a branch of the RCA in 60% of people.<br><br>Take Notes: SA node → RCA supply in 60%, LCX in 40% | SA node artery → right heart dominance determines supply | AV node → at base of interatrial septum, above coronary sinus os | His bundle → passes through membranous septum | Right coronary artery → dominant in 85%, supplies SA node, AV node, inferior LV",t:180},
  {q:"Glucagon secretion is stimulated by hypoglycaemia. Its primary metabolic target and effect is:",a:"Adipose tissue, causing lipolysis and ketogenesis",b:"Kidney, stimulating gluconeogenesis from glutamine",c:"Muscle, causing glycogen breakdown",d:"Liver, stimulating glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis via cAMP-PKA",ans:"D",e:"Glucagon acts EXCLUSIVELY on the liver (hepatocytes have glucagon receptors; muscle and adipose do not) - it binds glucagon receptors → Gs → adenylyl cyclase → cAMP → PKA → phosphorylation of glycogen phosphorylase (activates) and glycogen synthase (inhibits).<br><br>Take Notes: Glucagon → liver only (Gs-cAMP-PKA) | Stimulates → glycogenolysis + gluconeogenesis + lipolysis (indirect via adiponectin) + ketogenesis | Inhibits → glycogen synthesis | Glucagonoma → hyperglycaemia + necrolytic migratory erythema + weight loss | Treatment → somatostatin analogues",t:180},
  {q:"The concept of 'dead space' in pulmonary physiology - anatomical dead space is approximately:",a:"50 mL",b:"300 mL",c:"500 mL",d:"150 mL (roughly 1 mL per pound of body weight)",ans:"D",e:"Anatomical dead space is approximately 150 mL (about 1 mL/pound ideal body weight) - it represents the volume of the conducting airways that do not participate in gas exchange (trachea, bronchi, bronchioles down to the terminal bronchioles).<br><br>Take Notes: Anatomical dead space → 150 mL in adults | Alveolar dead space → alveoli ventilated but not perfused (V/Q = infinity) | Physiological dead space = anatomical + alveolar | Fowler's method → measures anatomical dead space (nitrogen washout) | Bohr equation → measures physiological dead space | Dead space increased → PE, positive pressure ventilation",t:180},
  {q:"The membrane potential of a resting nerve cell is approximately -70 mV. This is due primarily to:",a:"High extracellular potassium",b:"Selective permeability of the membrane to potassium and the Na+/K+ ATPase pump",c:"High intracellular sodium",d:"Chloride influx maintaining the negative potential",ans:"B",e:"Resting membrane potential (~-70 mV) is set by the Nernst equilibrium potential for K+ (-90 mV) - K+ leaks out via resting K+ channels down its concentration gradient (set by Na+/K+ ATPase). Na+/K+ ATPase directly contributes about -4 mV by pumping 3 Na+ out for 2 K+ in.<br><br>Take Notes: K+ equilibrium potential → -90 mV | Na+ equilibrium potential → +60 mV | Resting Vm → between these (-70 mV, closer to K+ as K+ permeability dominates at rest) | Na+/K+ ATPase → maintains concentration gradients | Hyperkalaemia → reduces concentration gradient → membrane potential becomes less negative → cell more excitable → arrhythmias",t:180},
  {q:"In the renal handling of glucose, the transport maximum (Tm) for glucose reabsorption in the proximal tubule is approximately:",a:"180 mg/dL (equal to the renal threshold for glycosuria)",b:"375 mg/min (maximum reabsorption rate, exceeded when plasma glucose is very high)",c:"250 mg/min",d:"500 mg/min",ans:"B",e:"The transport maximum (Tm) for glucose in the proximal tubule is ~375 mg/min in adults - plasma glucose normally ~100 mg/dL × GFR 125 mL/min = 125 mg/min filtered, well below Tm. Glycosuria begins when filtered load exceeds Tm (plasma glucose consistently >~300 mg/dL).<br><br>Take Notes: Renal threshold for glucose → ~180 mg/dL (plasma concentration at which glycosuria begins due to tubular heterogeneity/splay) | Tm → ~375 mg/min (maximum reabsorptive capacity) | SGLT2 inhibitors → lower Tm deliberately → glycosuria at lower plasma glucose | Splay phenomenon → glycosuria occurs before Tm due to nephron heterogeneity",t:180},
  {q:"Wernicke's encephalopathy results from thiamine (B1) deficiency. The most important enzyme complex impaired is:",a:"Pyruvate carboxylase",b:"Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex (PDH)",c:"Isocitrate dehydrogenase",d:"Succinate dehydrogenase",ans:"B",e:"PDH requires TPP (thiamine pyrophosphate) as cofactor - thiamine deficiency blocks PDH activity, accumulating pyruvate and lactate. The brain and heart are most affected due to high metabolic demand and exclusive reliance on aerobic glucose oxidation.<br><br>Take Notes: Thiamine-dependent enzymes → PDH + alpha-KGDH + transketolase (PPP) | Wernicke triad → confusion + ophthalmoplegia + ataxia | Wernicke → give IV thiamine BEFORE glucose | Korsakoff → chronic anterograde amnesia (mammillary body damage) | Refeeding syndrome → thiamine depletion when carbohydrates given after starvation",t:180},
  {q:"The urine of a patient with phenylketonuria (PKU) has a musty/mousy odour due to the accumulation of:",a:"Homogentisic acid",b:"Phenylpyruvic acid",c:"Methylmalonic acid",d:"Orotic acid",ans:"B",e:"In PKU, excess phenylalanine is transaminated to phenylpyruvate (the primary alternative pathway) which gives the urine a distinctive musty/mousy odour and is detectable on the Guthrie test (bacterial inhibition assay for elevated phenylalanine).<br><br>Take Notes: PKU → phenylalanine hydroxylase deficiency → phenylalanine → phenylpyruvate (musty urine) | Homogentisic acid → alkaptonuria (black urine on standing) | Methylmalonic acid → methylmalonic acidaemia | Orotic acid → OTC deficiency (urea cycle) | Neonatal screening → Guthrie test (heel prick)",t:180},
  {q:"The enzyme deficiency in classic homocystinuria (elevated homocysteine in urine and blood) is:",a:"Methionine synthase",b:"Cystathionine beta-synthase (CBS)",c:"Homocysteine methyltransferase",d:"Methylenetetrahydrofolate reductase (MTHFR)",ans:"B",e:"CBS converts homocysteine + serine → cystathionine. Deficiency → homocysteine accumulates → elevated plasma homocysteine → cross-linking of collagen → Marfanoid habitus, downward lens dislocation, thromboembolism, intellectual disability.<br><br>Take Notes: CBS deficiency → autosomal recessive, pyridoxine-responsive in 50% | Treatment → pyridoxine (B6) + methionine restriction + cysteine supplementation | Hyperhomocysteinaemia → independent cardiovascular risk factor | MTHFR mutation → mild hyperhomocysteinaemia (not classic homocystinuria) | Distinguish from Marfan → downward lens dislocation, intellectual disability, thrombosis",t:180},
  {q:"Glycogen synthase is activated by:",a:"Phosphorylation by PKA (cyclic AMP-dependent protein kinase)",b:"Dephosphorylation by protein phosphatase (insulin-stimulated)",c:"Glucagon stimulation",d:"Epinephrine stimulation",ans:"B",e:"Insulin activates protein phosphatase 1 (PP1) which dephosphorylates (and activates) glycogen synthase - the opposite of glucagon/epinephrine which phosphorylate and inactivate glycogen synthase via cAMP-PKA.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin → activates glycogen synthase (dephosphorylated = active) | Glucagon/epinephrine → PKA phosphorylates glycogen synthase → inactive | Reciprocal regulation → glycogen phosphorylase is active when glycogen synthase is inactive | Von Gierke disease → glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency (Type Ia GSD) → hypoglycaemia + hepatomegaly",t:180},
  {q:"In maple syrup urine disease (MSUD), the enzyme deficient is:",a:"Phenylalanine hydroxylase",b:"Branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase (BCKAD)",c:"Homogentisate oxidase",d:"Propionyl-CoA carboxylase",ans:"B",e:"MSUD results from BCKAD deficiency - this enzyme complex (requiring the same 5 cofactors as PDH and alpha-KGDH: TPP, lipoic acid, FAD, NAD+, CoA) is needed to oxidatively decarboxylate the keto acids of leucine, isoleucine, and valine, leading to their accumulation.<br><br>Take Notes: MSUD → accumulation of branched-chain amino acids and their keto acids | Sweet/maple syrup odour → from keto acid accumulation in urine and cerumen | Features → feeding difficulties, encephalopathy, seizures, neonatal period | Treatment → dietary restriction of Leu, Ile, Val + thiamine supplementation | Same cofactors as PDH → thiamine helps some patients (thiamine-responsive MSUD)",t:180},
  {q:"Alpha-1 antitrypsin (A1AT) deficiency causes emphysema by:",a:"Direct toxicity of the abnormal protein to alveolar cells",b:"Uninhibited neutrophil elastase destroying alveolar walls",c:"Recurrent pneumonias from impaired immune defence",d:"Increased mucus production obstructing small airways",ans:"B",e:"A1AT (a serine protease inhibitor/serpin) normally inhibits neutrophil elastase in the lung - deficiency allows elastase to destroy alveolar walls causing panacinar emphysema (especially at the base of the lungs, since A1AT is depleted by smoke in upper zones).<br><br>Take Notes: A1AT deficiency → panacinar emphysema (lower lobes predominant) + liver disease (Z allele protein accumulates in hepatocytes → cirrhosis) | PiZZ → severe deficiency | COPD-usual pattern → centrilobular (upper lobe) from smoking | Treatment → A1AT augmentation therapy (IV weekly) | Diagnosis → serum A1AT level + Pi typing (isoelectric focusing)",t:180},
  {q:"Which sphingolipidosis is characterised by 'cherry red spot' on fundoscopy and results from hexosaminidase A deficiency?",a:"Niemann-Pick disease",b:"Gaucher disease",c:"Tay-Sachs disease",d:"Fabry disease",ans:"C",e:"Tay-Sachs results from hexosaminidase A deficiency → GM2 ganglioside accumulates in neurons. The cherry red spot (fovea centralis appears red against a pale macula infiltrated with GM2) combined with progressive neurodegeneration and blindness in an Ashkenazi Jewish infant is the classic presentation.<br><br>Take Notes: Tay-Sachs → Hex A deficiency → GM2 accumulation → cherry red spot + neurodegeneration | Niemann-Pick → sphingomyelinase deficiency → sphingomyelin accumulation → also cherry red spot (type A/B) | Gaucher → glucocerebrosidase → most common sphingolipidosis, Gaucher cells (crumpled tissue paper) | Fabry → alpha-galactosidase A → globotriaosylceramide accumulation → painful neuropathy, angiokeratomas, renal failure",t:180},
  {q:"The conversion of pyruvate to acetyl-CoA by the PDH complex is irreversible. Why cannot acetyl-CoA be converted back to glucose?",a:"Acetyl-CoA lacks the necessary enzymes for gluconeogenesis",b:"There is no enzyme to reverse the CO2-losing decarboxylation step - acetyl-CoA cannot enter gluconeogenesis directly",c:"Acetyl-CoA is immediately oxidised in the TCA cycle",d:"The reaction requires ATP which is not available in gluconeogenic conditions",ans:"B",e:"The decarboxylation of pyruvate to acetyl-CoA is thermodynamically irreversible - the CO2 released cannot be recaptured to regenerate pyruvate. Therefore, acetyl-CoA cannot contribute net carbons to gluconeogenesis (only ketone bodies or lipids, not glucose).<br><br>Take Notes: Irreversible steps of glycolysis/TCA → hexokinase, PFK-1, pyruvate kinase, PDH | Acetyl-CoA → enters TCA (both carbons lost as CO2) | Odd-chain fatty acids → propionyl-CoA → succinyl-CoA → CAN enter gluconeogenesis | Amino acids → glucogenic ones enter as TCA intermediates or pyruvate | Fatty acids (even-chain) → CANNOT be converted to glucose",t:180},
  {q:"Orotic aciduria (elevated urinary orotate) occurs in which metabolic disorder?",a:"OTC (ornithine transcarbamylase) deficiency",b:"Gout (hyperuricaemia)",c:"PKU",d:"MSUD",ans:"A",e:"In OTC deficiency, carbamoyl phosphate accumulates in the mitochondria and spills into the cytoplasm where it enters the pyrimidine synthesis pathway → orotic acid accumulates. OTC deficiency → hyperammonaemia (post-protein challenge) + orotic aciduria + LOW blood citrulline.<br><br>Take Notes: OTC deficiency → X-linked, most common urea cycle defect | Orotic aciduria → OTC deficiency + CPSII deficiency + Lesch-Nyhan | No orotic aciduria → CPS1 deficiency, NAGS deficiency | Distinguish → OTC (orotic acid elevated, citrulline low) | Treatment → protein restriction + sodium benzoate/phenylbutyrate + arginine/citrulline supplementation",t:180},
  {q:"The limiting step in bile acid synthesis is the 7-alpha-hydroxylation of cholesterol, catalysed by:",a:"HMG-CoA reductase",b:"Cholesterol 7-alpha-hydroxylase (CYP7A1)",c:"Acyl-CoA:cholesterol acyltransferase (ACAT)",d:"Phospholipase A2",ans:"B",e:"CYP7A1 (cholesterol 7-alpha-hydroxylase) is the rate-limiting enzyme of primary bile acid synthesis in the liver - it is inhibited by bile acids returning via the enterohepatic circulation (feedback inhibition) and induced by cholesterol.<br><br>Take Notes: Bile acids → synthesised from cholesterol in liver | Rate-limiting step → CYP7A1 | Primary bile acids → cholic acid and chenodeoxycholic acid | Secondary bile acids → deoxycholic + lithocholic (from bacterial 7-dehydroxylation in colon) | Cholestyramine → sequesters bile acids → interrupts enterohepatic circulation → increases CYP7A1 → reduces plasma cholesterol",t:180},
  {q:"The concept of tumour grading versus staging: tumour GRADE reflects:",a:"Extent of spread to regional lymph nodes",b:"Differentiation of tumour cells relative to normal tissue of origin (histological)",c:"Size of the primary tumour only",d:"Presence or absence of distant metastases",ans:"B",e:"Grade is a histological concept - how closely the tumour cells resemble normal tissue (Grade 1 = well differentiated, Grade 3 = poorly differentiated/anaplastic). Stage (TNM) describes extent of disease spread. Grade and stage are independent prognostic variables.<br><br>Take Notes: Grade → histological differentiation (G1-G3) | Stage → extent of spread (TNM system) | High grade → aggressive, poor prognosis | Low grade → indolent, better prognosis | Some tumours → grade incorporated into stage (e.g., soft tissue sarcomas) | Gleason score → prostate cancer grading (sum of two most common patterns)",t:180},
  {q:"Virchow's triad predisposing to thrombosis - which component is most important in ARTERIAL thrombosis?",a:"Venous stasis",b:"Hypercoagulability",c:"Endothelial injury/dysfunction",d:"Turbulent flow alone",ans:"C",e:"Endothelial injury/dysfunction is the dominant factor in arterial thrombosis - atherosclerotic plaque disruption exposes subendothelial collagen and tissue factor, triggering platelet aggregation and coagulation cascade. Stasis is more important in venous thrombosis.<br><br>Take Notes: Arterial thrombosis → endothelial injury most important | Venous thrombosis → stasis + hypercoagulability most important | Endothelial injury exposes → collagen (platelet adhesion via GPIb-vWF) + tissue factor (extrinsic pathway) | Arteriosclerosis → endothelial dysfunction precedes plaque rupture | Virchow's triad → all three present in MI/PE but different dominance",t:180},
  {q:"The hallmark finding of coagulative necrosis (as in myocardial infarction) on histology is:",a:"Complete loss of cellular architecture with ghost cell outlines preserved",b:"Ghost outlines of cells preserved with cytoplasmic eosinophilia and loss of nuclei",c:"Liquefaction of all cellular components",d:"Granuloma formation with multinucleated giant cells",ans:"B",e:"In coagulative necrosis, protein denaturation (coagulation) preserves the tissue architecture - ghost outlines of cells remain visible with eosinophilic cytoplasm and pyknotic/karyolytic nuclei, while the overall shape of the organ is maintained. This is the most common form of necrosis.<br><br>Take Notes: Coagulative → ischaemia (most organs) - cell outlines preserved | Liquefactive → brain infarction + abscess (proteolytic enzymes dissolve all) | Caseous → TB + fungi (amorphous, no structure) | Fat → pancreatitis (saponification) | Fibrinoid → autoimmune vasculitis (immune complex deposition + fibrin in vessel walls)",t:180},
  {q:"The pathological diagnosis of malignant vs benign melanocytic tumours most relies on which histological features?",a:"Cell size alone",b:"Asymmetry + border irregularity + colour variation on dermoscopy (clinical, not histological)",c:"Histological evidence of invasion of dermis with atypical melanocytes, high mitotic rate, and lack of maturation with depth",d:"Presence of melanin pigment",ans:"C",e:"Malignant melanoma histologically shows: dermal invasion by atypical melanocytes (pagetoid spread), high mitotic rate, lack of maturation with depth (cells remain large as they go deeper, unlike benign naevi which become smaller/'mature').<br><br>Take Notes: Melanoma histological features → pagetoid spread + dermal invasion + atypia + mitoses + no maturation | Clark levels → depth of invasion (I-V) | Breslow thickness → most important prognostic factor (mm from granular layer to deepest tumour cell) | Sentinel node biopsy → if Breslow >1mm (or 0.8mm with adverse features) | BRAF V600E mutation → 50% of melanomas; targeted by vemurafenib",t:180},
  {q:"Type IV hypersensitivity (delayed type, T-cell mediated) is characteristically seen in:",a:"Anaphylaxis and urticaria",b:"Serum sickness and post-streptococcal GN",c:"Haemolytic transfusion reactions",d:"Contact dermatitis (e.g., nickel allergy) and TB tuberculin skin test",ans:"D",e:"Type IV (delayed type) hypersensitivity is T-lymphocyte-mediated (not antibody-mediated) - sensitised CD4+ T cells release cytokines and activate macrophages 48-72 hours after antigen re-exposure, causing granulomatous inflammation or eczematous contact dermatitis.<br><br>Take Notes: Type I → IgE, mast cells, immediate (anaphylaxis, asthma, urticaria) | Type II → IgG/IgM, direct cytotoxicity (haemolytic anaemia, Goodpasture, pemphigus) | Type III → immune complex deposition (serum sickness, SLE, post-strep GN) | Type IV → T-cell, delayed 48-72h (contact dermatitis, tuberculin test, transplant rejection, MS, T1DM) | Patch test → diagnoses contact allergy (type IV)",t:180},
  {q:"The Philadelphia chromosome (t9;22) translocation results in BCR-ABL fusion. In CML, the p210 BCR-ABL differs from the p190 BCR-ABL seen in Ph+ ALL because:",a:"p190 has stronger constitutive tyrosine kinase activity, causing more aggressive disease (Ph+ ALL)",b:"p210 has stronger tyrosine kinase activity than p190",c:"p190 is a larger protein than p210",d:"p190 is associated with CML and p210 with ALL",ans:"A",e:"p190 BCR-ABL (from the minor breakpoint cluster region) has stronger constitutive tyrosine kinase activity and causes more aggressive disease (Ph+ ALL, ~25% of adult ALL) compared to p210 (from major BCR, causes CML and ~5% of adult ALL).<br><br>Take Notes: t(9;22) → Philadelphia chromosome | p210 → typical CML breakpoint (major BCR) | p190 → Ph+ ALL (minor BCR), more aggressive | Both respond to imatinib but p190 ALL is more resistant | CML phases → chronic → accelerated → blast crisis | Blast crisis → >20% blasts in marrow; resembles AML (70%) or ALL (30%)",t:180},
  {q:"Mesothelioma is characteristically associated with asbestos exposure. The MOST oncogenic asbestos type is:",a:"Chrysotile (white asbestos, serpentine)",b:"Amosite (brown amphibole asbestos)",c:"Crocidolite (blue asbestos, amphibole)",d:"Tremolite",ans:"C",e:"Crocidolite (blue asbestos, amphibole type) is the most oncogenic asbestos type for mesothelioma - its sharp needle-like fibres penetrate deeply into pleural mesothelium, resist macrophage clearance, and activate the NLRP3 inflammasome causing chronic inflammation.<br><br>Take Notes: Amphibole fibres (crocidolite, amosite) → highest mesothelioma risk | Chrysotile (serpentine) → more curly, more easily cleared from lungs, lower mesothelioma risk but still carcinogenic | Mesothelioma latency → 20-40 years | Markers → calretinin, WT1, CK5/6, D2-40 (mesothelioma) | Prognosis → median survival 12-18 months",t:180},
  {q:"In cystic fibrosis, the CFTR protein mutation deltaF508 causes disease by:",a:"Producing a truncated protein with abnormal function",b:"Causing CFTR protein misfolding and premature degradation in the ER (Class II mutation: protein trafficking defect)",c:"Preventing CFTR gene transcription",d:"Creating a splicing error that deletes the ion channel domain",ans:"B",e:"DeltaF508 (deletion of phenylalanine at position 508) is a Class II CFTR mutation - the protein is produced but misfolded, recognized by ER quality control (calnexin), and targeted for proteasomal degradation before reaching the apical cell membrane.<br><br>Take Notes: CFTR Class II (deltaF508) → most common (70% of CF alleles) | Elexacaftor/tezacaftor/ivacaftor (Trikafta) → corrector + potentiator → most effective for F508del | Class III (G551D) → gating defect (protein reaches membrane but channel doesn't open properly) | Ivacaftor → potentiator only (for gating defects) | Sweat chloride >60 mEq/L diagnostic",t:180},
  {q:"The most common inherited thrombophilia (hypercoagulable state) in Europe is:",a:"Protein C deficiency",b:"Antithrombin III deficiency",c:"Factor V Leiden mutation (activated protein C resistance)",d:"Prothrombin G20210A mutation",ans:"C",e:"Factor V Leiden (missense mutation R506Q preventing cleavage of Factor V by activated protein C) is the most common inherited thrombophilia, found in ~5% of the European population and up to 20% of patients with venous thromboembolism.<br><br>Take Notes: Factor V Leiden → cannot be inactivated by APC → Factor V remains active → hypercoagulability | Prevalence → 5% Europeans, 20-40% of VTE patients | Autosomal dominant | Heterozygous → 3-7× VTE risk | Homozygous → 25-50× VTE risk | Test → APC resistance ratio or gene analysis | Second most common → Prothrombin G20210A mutation (2-3% Europeans)",t:180},
  {q:"Nosocomial bloodstream infections in ICU patients are most commonly caused by:",a:"Gram-negative rods (E. coli, Klebsiella)",b:"Coagulase-negative staphylococci (e.g., Staph epidermidis) from central line contamination",c:"Streptococcus pyogenes",d:"Candida species only",ans:"B",e:"Coagulase-negative staphylococci (especially S. epidermidis) are the most common cause of catheter-related bloodstream infections (CRBSI) in ICUs - they form biofilms on central venous catheters resistant to most antibiotics.<br><br>Take Notes: CoNS CRBSI → S. epidermidis most common | Biofilm on central lines → antibiotic resistance | Treatment → vancomycin + catheter removal | Community-acquired bacteraemia → S. aureus most common overall | Gram-negative CRBSI → less common but more mortality | Prevention → sterile technique + chlorhexidine skin prep + hand hygiene + early line removal",t:180},
  {q:"The Weil-Felix reaction (agglutination of Proteus strains) is a historical serological test for:",a:"Typhoid fever",b:"Rickettsial infections",c:"Leptospirosis",d:"Brucellosis",ans:"B",e:"The Weil-Felix test uses cross-reactive antigens between Rickettsia species and Proteus OX strains - Proteus OX19, OX2, and OXK strains agglutinate with sera from patients with different rickettsial diseases. Now largely replaced by more specific immunofluorescent antibody (IFA) tests.<br><br>Take Notes: Rickettsial diseases → obligate intracellular gram-negative bacteria | Rocky Mountain spotted fever → R. rickettsii, OX19 positive | Typhus → OX19 positive | Tsutsugamushi (scrub typhus) → OX-K positive | Weil-Felix → non-specific, now replaced by IFA | Weil's disease → Leptospirosis (different test: MSAT)",t:180},
  {q:"Cryptococcus neoformans infection in AIDS patients presents as meningitis with a characteristic CSF finding of:",a:"Numerous neutrophils and low glucose",b:"Very few cells (lymphocytes) with high opening pressure and organisms visible on India ink staining",c:"Xanthochromia with elevated protein",d:"Eosinophilic pleocytosis",ans:"B",e:"Cryptococcal meningitis in AIDS (CD4 <100) is characterised by surprisingly few CSF cells (minimal inflammation because of immune deficiency), high opening pressure (from polysaccharide capsule impeding CSF reabsorption), and India ink staining shows encapsulated yeast (large capsule halo).<br><br>Take Notes: India ink stain → reveals capsule as clear halo | Cryptococcal antigen (CrAg) → most sensitive (>99%) | Treatment → amphotericin B + flucytosine (induction 2 weeks) → fluconazole consolidation → lifelong prophylaxis until CD4 >200 | High ICP → serial lumbar punctures or VP shunt | Screen for CrAg in all HIV patients with CD4 <100",t:180},
  {q:"Cholera toxin causes massive watery diarrhoea by:",a:"Directly lysing intestinal epithelial cells",b:"ADP-ribosylating the Gs protein, permanently activating adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP and causing Cl- secretion",c:"Inhibiting protein synthesis in enterocytes",d:"Blocking Na+ absorption in the large bowel",ans:"B",e:"Cholera toxin (A subunit) ADP-ribosylates the alpha subunit of Gs protein, locking it in the active GTP-bound conformation → permanently elevated adenylyl cyclase → high cAMP → PKA → phosphorylation of CFTR → massive Cl- and water secretion into the intestinal lumen.<br><br>Take Notes: Cholera toxin mechanism → Gs → cAMP → CFTR chloride secretion | Pertussis toxin → ADP-ribosylates Gi (opposite effect) | E. coli LT (heat-labile) toxin → same mechanism as cholera toxin | ST (heat-stable) toxin → activates guanylyl cyclase → cGMP → inhibits NaCl absorption | Treatment → oral rehydration solution (rice water ORS) ± azithromycin",t:180},
  {q:"The most important virulence factor of Streptococcus pyogenes (Group A Strep) that protects against phagocytosis is:",a:"Streptolysin O (antistreptolysin O, ASO titre measurement target)",b:"M protein (antiphagocytic surface protein, basis of serotyping)",c:"Streptokinase",d:"Hyaluronidase (spreading factor)",ans:"B",e:"M protein is the principal virulence factor of GAS - it interferes with complement-mediated opsonisation by binding factor H (complement regulatory protein) and fibrinogen, preventing C3b deposition and phagocytosis. Antibodies to M protein confer type-specific immunity.<br><br>Take Notes: M protein → antiphagocytic, basis of Lancefield group A serotyping | Over 180 M types | Molecular mimicry → M protein cross-reacts with cardiac myosin (rheumatic fever) | ASO titre → antibody to streptolysin O (rises after pharyngeal GAS infection) | GAS pharyngitis → treat with penicillin (prevents rheumatic fever) | Pyoderma (impetigo) → GAS does not cause rheumatic fever from skin infection",t:180},
  {q:"Pharmacokinetics describes what the body does to the drug. Which parameter determines how frequently a drug should be dosed?",a:"Volume of distribution",b:"Protein binding",c:"Half-life (determines dosing interval)",d:"Bioavailability",ans:"C",e:"The half-life (t½) determines how frequently a drug must be dosed to maintain therapeutic levels - dosing intervals of 1-2 half-lives prevent excessive accumulation while maintaining efficacy. Steady state is reached in 4-5 half-lives regardless of dose or frequency.<br><br>Take Notes: t½ → determines dosing interval and time to steady state | Vd → determines loading dose | Clearance → determines maintenance dose | Steady state → reached in 4-5 half-lives | Half-life = 0.693 × Vd / Cl",t:180},
  {q:"A first-generation antihistamine (diphenhydramine) causes sedation because it:",a:"Stimulates H1 receptors in the CNS",b:"Crosses the blood-brain barrier and blocks central H1 receptors (which normally promote wakefulness)",c:"Stimulates muscarinic receptors centrally",d:"Inhibits serotonin reuptake",ans:"B",e:"Central H1 receptors in the tuberomammillary nucleus promote wakefulness - their blockade by lipophilic first-generation antihistamines (which cross the BBB) causes sedation. Second-generation antihistamines (cetirizine, loratadine) are poorly CNS-penetrant and non-sedating.<br><br>Take Notes: First-gen antihistamines → sedating (diphenhydramine, chlorphenamine, promethazine) | Second-gen → non-sedating (cetirizine, loratadine, fexofenadine) | First-gen also → antimuscarinic (dry mouth, urinary retention, blurred vision) | H2 blockers → ranitidine, famotidine (reduce gastric acid, not for allergy) | Motion sickness → promethazine/cyclizine (anticholinergic/H1 effect)",t:180},
  {q:"The mechanism of action of sodium valproate (valproic acid) in epilepsy includes:",a:"Selective enhancement of GABA-A receptor activity only",b:"Multiple mechanisms: blocks voltage-gated Na+ channels + increases GABA (by inhibiting GABA transaminase) + blocks T-type calcium channels",c:"Pure blockade of glutamate receptors",d:"Enhancement of glycine-mediated inhibition only",ans:"B",e:"Valproate has multiple mechanisms: blocks voltage-gated Na+ channels (like phenytoin), increases brain GABA by inhibiting GABA-transaminase (GABA catabolism enzyme) and succinic semialdehyde dehydrogenase, and blocks T-type calcium channels (important in absence seizures).<br><br>Take Notes: Broad-spectrum AED → effective in all seizure types (partial, generalised, absence) | Teratogenic → neural tube defects (folic acid supplement essential) | Side effects → hepatotoxicity, pancreatitis, weight gain, alopecia, tremor, thrombocytopenia | Blocks GABA transaminase → increases synaptic GABA | Pregnancy → highest teratogenicity of all AEDs (avoid or use with folate)",t:180},
  {q:"Suxamethonium (succinylcholine) is a depolarising neuromuscular blocking agent. It causes fasciculations and then paralysis because:",a:"It stimulates nicotinic receptors continuously preventing repolarisation (Phase I block)",b:"It blocks nicotinic receptors competitively at the NMJ",c:"It inhibits acetylcholinesterase at the NMJ",d:"It depletes ACh from the motor nerve terminal",ans:"A",e:"Suxamethonium binds nicotinic receptors and causes persistent depolarisation of the end-plate (Phase I block) - initial fasciculations occur from repetitive firing, but sustained depolarisation prevents repolarisation needed for another action potential, causing flaccid paralysis.<br><br>Take Notes: Phase I block → depolarising, not reversed by neostigmine (worsens) | Phase II block → with prolonged exposure, becomes non-depolarising (can be partially reversed) | Contraindications → hyperkalaemia (burns >24h, denervation, rhabdomyolysis), malignant hyperthermia susceptibility, pseudocholinesterase deficiency | Reversed by → pseudocholinesterase (not neostigmine) | Only depolarising NMJ blocker available",t:180},
  {q:"A 50-year-old man with chronic renal failure (GFR 15 mL/min) needs pain relief post-surgery. Which opioid requires the most careful dose reduction due to accumulation of active renally-cleared metabolites?",a:"Fentanyl",b:"Morphine (morphine-6-glucuronide accumulates causing respiratory depression)",c:"Tramadol",d:"Buprenorphine",ans:"B",e:"Morphine is metabolised to morphine-6-glucuronide (M6G, potent opioid agonist) and morphine-3-glucuronide (M3G, neuroexcitatory) - both are renally cleared and accumulate in renal failure causing prolonged respiratory depression and CNS toxicity.<br><br>Take Notes: Morphine in renal failure → accumulation of M6G → respiratory depression | Fentanyl → preferred in renal failure (inactive metabolites) | Hydromorphone → also usable in renal failure | Codeine → prodrug of morphine, also avoid in renal failure | Tramadol → active O-desmethyl metabolite accumulates in renal failure (seizures) | Buprenorphine → hepatic metabolism, safer in renal failure",t:180},
  {q:"Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT) type II is an immune-mediated disorder. The initial treatment after stopping all heparin is:",a:"Platelet transfusion to prevent bleeding",b:"Warfarin alone as alternative anticoagulation",c:"Direct thrombin inhibitor (argatroban) or fondaparinux",d:"Fresh frozen plasma",ans:"C",e:"In HIT type II, after stopping all heparin (including LMWH, heparin flushes, heparin-coated catheters), a non-heparin anticoagulant is essential because the prothrombotic state persists - argatroban (hepatic clearance, safe in renal failure) or fondaparinux are first-line.<br><br>Take Notes: HIT antibodies → against PF4-heparin complex | 4T score → Thrombocytopenia, Timing (days 5-10), Thrombosis, oTher cause excluded | Platelet transfusion contraindicated (fuels thrombosis) | Warfarin NOT initially (worsens by depleting protein C) | Argatroban → direct thrombin inhibitor, hepatically cleared | Transition to warfarin → only after platelet count recovered >150 × 10⁹/L",t:180},
  {q:"The proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) irreversibly inhibit the H+/K+-ATPase. The duration of acid suppression exceeds the plasma half-life (1-2 hours) because:",a:"They accumulate in the stomach mucosa",b:"They are converted to active metabolites with longer half-lives",c:"New H+/K+-ATPase must be synthesised (takes 18-24 hours) after irreversible inhibition",d:"They have active enterohepatic recycling",ans:"C",e:"PPIs are irreversible inhibitors - once they bind and inactivate the proton pump, acid suppression lasts until new pump protein is synthesised (18-24 hours). This explains the prolonged acid suppression despite short plasma half-lives and the need for consistent once-daily morning dosing.<br><br>Take Notes: PPIs → activate in acidic canaliculus → irreversible inhibition of H+/K+-ATPase | Take 30-60 min before meals (pump must be active to be inhibited) | Takes 2-5 days for full effect (replace existing pumps) | Best given morning before breakfast | Interactions → reduce clopidogrel activation (via CYP2C19) | Long-term → hypomagnesaemia, C. diff risk, bone fractures",t:180},
  {q:"Tamoxifen is used for hormone receptor-positive breast cancer. Its mechanism of action in breast tissue is:",a:"Direct destruction of oestrogen in breast tissue",b:"Competitive antagonist at oestrogen receptors in breast (while acting as partial agonist in uterus and bone)",c:"Inhibition of aromatase enzyme reducing oestrogen synthesis",d:"Downregulation of HER2 receptors",ans:"B",e:"Tamoxifen is a selective oestrogen receptor modulator (SERM) - it competitively blocks ER in breast (antioestrogen effect → inhibits tumour growth) but acts as a partial agonist in uterus (increased endometrial cancer risk) and bone (preserves bone density).<br><br>Take Notes: Tamoxifen → SERM, ER antagonist in breast | Side effects → hot flushes, endometrial cancer (2-3× risk), DVT, cataracts | Aromatase inhibitors (anastrozole, letrozole) → inhibit peripheral oestrogen synthesis, used post-menopause | Fulvestrant → ER downregulator (no agonist activity anywhere) | Pre-menopausal → tamoxifen preferred (aromatase inhibitors don't work if ovaries active) | 5 years → standard; 10 years → reduces recurrence further",t:180},
  {q:"Metronidazole is commonly used for Clostridium difficile infection. However, oral vancomycin is preferred for which category of C. diff disease?",a:"Mild C. difficile colitis as first-line treatment",b:"Severe or complicated C. difficile infection (e.g., WBC >15,000, creatinine >1.5× baseline, or hypotension)",c:"Asymptomatic C. difficile carriers",d:"Prophylaxis in patients on antibiotics",ans:"B",e:"Oral vancomycin (or fidaxomicin) is preferred over metronidazole for severe C. difficile infection (IDSA criteria: WBC >15,000 or creatinine ≥1.5× premorbid) due to higher luminal concentrations and superior efficacy in severe disease.<br><br>Take Notes: C. diff severity criteria → WBC >15,000 + OR creatinine ≥1.5× OR hypotension/shock/ileus/megacolon | Mild-moderate → fidaxomicin (preferred for recurrence prevention) or oral vancomycin | Severe complicated → oral vancomycin + IV metronidazole ± surgery | Fidaxomicin → narrow spectrum, reduces recurrence rates | FMT (faecal microbiota transplant) → recurrent CDI (≥2 recurrences)",t:180},
  {q:"A 30-year-old male with HIV on antiretroviral therapy develops a painful vesicular rash in a thoracic dermatomal distribution. The prophylaxis that should have been used to prevent this reactivation is:",a:"Acyclovir 400mg twice daily when CD4 <200",b:"Valaciclovir 500mg once daily is recommended for VZV prophylaxis only if CD4 <50",c:"Recombinant zoster vaccine (Shingrix) when CD4 count allows",d:"There is no evidence-based prophylaxis for VZV reactivation in HIV",ans:"C",e:"Shingrix (recombinant, non-live zoster vaccine) is recommended for immunocompromised patients including those with HIV, as it can be given regardless of CD4 count - it reduces herpes zoster incidence and severity. Live attenuated zoster vaccine is contraindicated in immunocompromised patients.<br><br>Take Notes: Recombinant Shingrix → non-live, can be given to immunocompromised | Live zoster vaccine (Zostavax) → contraindicated in HIV or immunosuppressed | Shingrix two doses → given 2-6 months apart | VZV reactivation → increased risk with CD4 <200 | Treatment of zoster → IV acyclovir in HIV (high dose, may need hospitalisation) | Prophylaxis with acyclovir → used for preventing VZV recurrence post-treatment, not standard primary prophylaxis",t:180},
  {q:"Community medicine: The positive predictive value of a test INCREASES when:",a:"Sensitivity is increased while specificity remains the same",b:"Specificity of the test is increased",c:"The prevalence of the disease in the population tested increases",d:"The cut-off threshold of the test is lowered",ans:"C",e:"PPV = TP / (TP + FP) - as disease prevalence rises in the tested population, a greater proportion of positives are true positives, directly increasing PPV. Specificity and sensitivity are intrinsic test properties that do not change with prevalence.<br><br>Take Notes: PPV increases with → rising prevalence + increased specificity | NPV increases with → falling prevalence + increased sensitivity | Screening low-prevalence populations → low PPV (many false positives) | High-prevalence populations (e.g., ICU patients) → higher PPV | Bayesian reasoning → pre-test probability (prevalence) × test characteristics = post-test probability",t:180},
  {q:"Behavioural sciences: The Kubler-Ross stage of grief most associated with retrospective self-blame and 'if only' statements is:",a:"Denial",b:"Anger",c:"Bargaining",d:"Depression",ans:"C",e:"The bargaining stage involves retrospective negotiation - 'if only I had noticed sooner', 'if only I hadn't smoked', making deals with God or fate. It represents an attempt to regain control or delay the inevitable through negotiation.<br><br>Take Notes: Bargaining → 'if only', 'what if', retrospective guilt, magical thinking | Denial → protective buffer, first stage | Anger → 'why me?', displaced rage | Depression → preparatory grief, withdrawal, sadness | Acceptance → not happiness but acknowledgement | Not all patients experience all stages or in order",t:180},
  {q:"Biostatistics: The number needed to treat (NNT) is calculated as:",a:"Relative risk reduction (RRR) × 100",b:"1 / Absolute risk reduction (ARR)",c:"Incidence in treated / incidence in control",d:"Odds ratio × prevalence",ans:"B",e:"NNT = 1 / ARR where ARR = control event rate - treatment event rate. NNT represents the number of patients who need to receive the treatment to prevent one additional adverse outcome compared to control.<br><br>Take Notes: NNT = 1/ARR | ARR = control rate - treatment rate (absolute difference) | RRR = ARR / control rate (relative difference) | Lower NNT = more effective treatment | NNT 1 = 100% effective (treat 1 patient, prevent 1 event) | NNH (number needed to harm) = 1/ARI (absolute risk increase)",t:180},
  {q:"A drug has a volume of distribution (Vd) of 350 L in a 70 kg adult. This suggests:",a:"The drug is confined to the plasma compartment",b:"The drug is heavily bound to tissue proteins and extensively distributed beyond plasma",c:"The drug has poor oral bioavailability",d:"The drug is rapidly eliminated",ans:"B",e:"A large Vd (>5 L/kg = 350 L in 70 kg patient) indicates extensive tissue distribution - the drug is concentrated in tissues (often bound to tissue proteins or lipids) and a low plasma concentration per dose. Plasma water = 3L, blood = 5L, ECF = 15L, total body water = 42L.<br><br>Take Notes: Vd <5L → plasma compartment (warfarin, heparin) | Vd 5-20L → ECF (aminoglycosides) | Vd >40L → total body water (ethanol) | Vd >200-400L → extensive tissue binding (chloroquine, digoxin, amiodarone) | Loading dose = Target Cp × Vd | High Vd → not easily dialysed",t:180},
  {q:"The most sensitive marker of perioperative myocardial injury after non-cardiac surgery is:",a:"CK-MB isoenzyme",b:"Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH)",c:"High-sensitivity troponin (hs-cTnI or hs-cTnT)",d:"ECG changes alone",ans:"C",e:"High-sensitivity troponin is the most sensitive and specific biomarker for myocardial injury - it can detect myocardial cell necrosis within 1-3 hours and is elevated in perioperative myocardial injury (MINS) even without classic symptoms, predicting 30-day mortality.<br><br>Take Notes: MINS → myocardial injury after non-cardiac surgery (troponin rise without other clinical criteria) | High-sens troponin → detects very small amounts of myocardial injury | CK-MB → older, less sensitive, also elevated in skeletal muscle injury | Perioperative MI → peak incidence 24-48h post-surgery | Serial troponins → within 48h post high-risk surgery",t:180},
  {q:"In the management of acute pancreatitis, early enteral nutrition (within 24-48 hours) is preferred over parenteral nutrition because:",a:"It is cheaper and easier to administer",b:"It maintains gut barrier function, reduces bacterial translocation, and reduces infectious complications",c:"Parenteral nutrition causes pancreatitis to worsen",d:"Enteral nutrition suppresses pancreatic exocrine secretion more effectively",ans:"B",e:"Early enteral nutrition (preferably nasojejunal, though nasogastric is acceptable) maintains enterocyte integrity, preserves gut microbiota, and prevents bacterial translocation from the gut lumen to the inflamed peripancreatic tissue - reducing infectious complications of necrotising pancreatitis.<br><br>Take Notes: Enteral > parenteral in acute pancreatitis | Nasogastric route → acceptable if nasojejunal not feasible | TPN only → if enteral route not possible (ileus) | 'Gut rest' (NPO) → outdated for severe pancreatitis | NICE guideline → offer enteral tube feeding within 24h of admission in predicted severe pancreatitis",t:180},
  {q:"The systemic inflammatory response syndrome (SIRS) criteria include all of the following EXCEPT:",a:"Temperature >38°C or <36°C",b:"Heart rate >90 bpm",c:"Blood pressure <90 mmHg systolic",d:"White blood cell count >12,000 or <4,000 cells/μL",ans:"C",e:"The original SIRS criteria (2 or more): temperature >38°C or <36°C, HR >90, RR >20 or PaCO2 <32 mmHg, WBC >12,000 or <4,000 or >10% bands. Hypotension is NOT a SIRS criterion but is part of septic shock criteria (now Sepsis-3 criteria).<br><br>Take Notes: SIRS criteria (old, 2 of 4) → Temperature + HR + RR/PaCO2 + WBC | Sepsis-3 (2016) → infection + organ dysfunction (SOFA score ≥2) | Septic shock → sepsis + vasopressors to maintain MAP ≥65 + lactate >2 mmol/L despite fluids | qSOFA → RR ≥22 + altered mentation + SBP ≤100 (bedside screening) | Blood pressure <90 → defines hypotension/septic shock",t:180},
  {q:"A drain inserted in the Morrison's pouch (hepatorenal space) after a laparoscopic cholecystectomy drains from which anatomical space?",a:"Lesser sac",b:"Right subphrenic space",c:"Right subhepatic space (hepatorenal space/Morrison's pouch)",d:"Prevesical space",ans:"C",e:"Morrison's pouch (hepatorenal recess) is the deepest part of the peritoneal cavity in the supine position - it lies between the inferior surface of the right lobe of the liver and the right kidney, and is the dependent site where blood or bile collects after right upper quadrant surgery.<br><br>Take Notes: Morrison's pouch → right subhepatic (between liver and right kidney) → most dependent supine position | Most common site for post-op fluid collection in RUQ surgery | Right subphrenic space → above liver, right of falciform ligament | FAST scan → Morrison's pouch is first examined for fluid | Pouch of Douglas → most dependent pelvic space (between rectum and uterus/bladder)",t:180},
  {q:"In laparoscopic surgery, the pneumoperitoneum is created using CO2 because:",a:"It is readily available and inexpensive only",b:"CO2 is rapidly absorbed into blood, highly soluble in plasma, non-flammable, minimally toxic, and exhaled via the lungs",c:"It causes less post-operative pain than air",d:"CO2 has lower diffusing capacity than other gases",ans:"B",e:"CO2 is the ideal insufflation gas - it is rapidly absorbed (20× more soluble in blood than nitrogen), metabolised and exhaled via the lungs, non-combustible (safe with electrosurgery), and has lower risk of gas embolism than air (CO2 emboli dissolve rapidly).<br><br>Take Notes: CO2 pneumoperitoneum → 12-15 mmHg pressure typically | Complications → hypercapnia (CO2 absorption), subcutaneous emphysema, CO2 embolism, physiological changes from pressure | CO2 embolism → mill-wheel murmur, haemodynamic collapse, treat with Trendelenburg left decubitus + aspiration | Pre-existing COPD → increased CO2 retention risk",t:180},
  {q:"Wound dehiscence (burst abdomen) most commonly occurs on which post-operative day?",a:"Days 1-2",b:"Day 5-8",c:"Day 14-21",d:"Days 1-2 and day 14-21 (bimodal)",ans:"B",e:"Wound dehiscence peaks at days 5-8 when wound tensile strength is at its minimum - the initial fibrin layer has been partially replaced by early disorganised collagen, but before mature collagen crosslinks develop. A tell-tale sign is sudden serosanguinous discharge from the wound.<br><br>Take Notes: Wound healing phases → inflammatory (0-4d), proliferative (4d-3wk), remodelling (3wk-2yr) | Tensile strength → 0% at time of closure, ~5% at day 1-4, minimum during proliferative disruption around day 5-8, then rising | Serosanguinous discharge → warning sign of impending dehiscence | Risk factors → infection, obesity, malnutrition, steroid use, haematoma, poor technique | Treatment → return to theatre for closure ± VAC dressing",t:180},
  {q:"During a Whipple procedure (pancreaticoduodenectomy), which artery must be preserved to ensure viability of the stomach remnant?",a:"Gastroduodenal artery",b:"Right gastric artery",c:"Right gastroepiploic artery",d:"Left gastric artery",ans:"D",e:"During pylorus-preserving or standard Whipple, the right gastric and right gastroepiploic arteries are typically divided. The stomach remnant survives on the left gastric artery and left gastroepiploic artery - the left gastric is the dominant supply and must be preserved.<br><br>Take Notes: Whipple → resects head of pancreas + duodenum + common bile duct distal segment + part of stomach (or preserves pylorus = PPPD) | Stomach remnant supply → left gastric artery (must preserve) | Pancreatic anastomosis → highest risk of leak (pancreaticojejunostomy) | Overall complication rate → 40-50% in high-volume centres | Delayed gastric emptying → most common complication",t:180},
  {q:"The difference between primary, secondary, and tertiary wound closure: secondary closure (healing by secondary intention) is used when:",a:"The wound is clean and primarily sutured",b:"The wound is infected, contaminated, or has tissue loss preventing primary closure - the wound is left open to granulate",c:"The wound is sutured 3-5 days after initial debridement",d:"Skin grafts are applied immediately",ans:"B",e:"Secondary intention healing allows infected or contaminated wounds to granulate from the base - bacteria counts must be <105 organisms/g before closure. Tertiary closure (delayed primary closure) is when a wound is initially left open and sutured 3-5 days later after confirming clean granulation tissue.<br><br>Take Notes: Primary closure → clean wound sutured immediately | Secondary intention → infected, contaminated, or tissue-loss wounds left to granulate | Tertiary (delayed primary) → wound left open 3-5 days then sutured after verification of healthy granulation | Granulation tissue → fibroblasts + new vessels + macrophages | Vacuum-assisted closure (VAC) → accelerates secondary intention healing",t:180},
  {q:"The most common cause of post-operative hypoxia in the immediate recovery room period (0-2 hours) is:",a:"Pulmonary embolism",b:"Residual effects of anaesthetic agents causing respiratory depression and/or airway obstruction",c:"Pneumonia",d:"Acute respiratory distress syndrome",ans:"B",e:"Residual anaesthetic effects (opioids, volatile agents, muscle relaxants) cause hypoventilation, upper airway obstruction (tongue falling back against pharyngeal wall, laryngospasm), and loss of hypoxic drive - these are the most common immediate post-anaesthetic causes of hypoxia.<br><br>Take Notes: Recovery room hypoxia → laryngospasm + airway obstruction + residual opioid + residual muscle relaxant | Laryngospasm → most dangerous (complete closure of vocal cords) → jaw thrust + CPAP + suxamethonium | Opioid reversal → naloxone 100-200 mcg IV | Muscle relaxant reversal → neostigmine + glycopyrrolate or sugammadex | PE/pneumonia → later complications",t:180},
  {q:"An anastomotic leak after colorectal surgery classically presents at which post-operative day?",a:"Days 1-2 (immediate)",b:"Day 5-7",c:"Day 14 or later",d:"Only if drain output changes",ans:"B",e:"Anastomotic leaks typically present at days 5-7 - by this time, suture strength is declining and bacterial invasion of the anastomosis occurs. Clinical signs: tachycardia, fever, abdominal pain, rising inflammatory markers, or peritonitis.<br><br>Take Notes: Anastomotic leak risk factors → proximal diversion absent, poor blood supply, tension on anastomosis, contamination, malnutrition, steroids, technical error | CRP >150 mg/L at day 3-5 → highly predictive of anastomotic leak | CT scan → investigation of choice | Management → small leak (drain + antibiotics) vs major leak (Hartmann's or re-anastomosis with defunctioning stoma) | Mortality → 10-20% with leak",t:180},
  {q:"Which of the following is the MOST accurate test to predict operative mortality in liver resection?",a:"Total bilirubin level",b:"Child-Pugh score",c:"MELD score only",d:"Indocyanine green clearance (ICG retention rate at 15 minutes) and future liver remnant volume",ans:"D",e:"ICG clearance (hepatic functional reserve) combined with future liver remnant volume (FLR, calculated on CT) are the most accurate predictors of post-hepatectomy liver failure - ICG R15 <10% and FLR >25-30% are standard thresholds for safe major hepatectomy.<br><br>Take Notes: ICG R15 → measures hepatocyte transport function | ICG R15 <10% → safe for major hepatectomy | ICG R15 >20% → only minor resection safe | FLR → should be >25-30% for normal liver (>40% for cirrhotic) | Portal vein embolisation (PVE) → used to increase FLR before major resection | Child-Pugh → cirrhosis assessment, not specific for resection",t:180},
  {q:"The most sensitive imaging modality for detecting liver metastases from colorectal cancer is:",a:"CT scan with contrast",b:"MRI liver with gadolinium and hepatocyte-specific contrast (Gd-EOB-DTPA/Primovist)",c:"PET-CT",d:"USS with contrast enhancement",ans:"B",e:"MRI with hepatocyte-specific contrast (Gadoxetate/Primovist) achieves hepatocyte-specific uptake in the hepatobiliary phase, allowing detection of metastases as small as 5-7 mm with very high sensitivity (>95%) - superior to CT for small lesions and lesions in fatty or cirrhotic livers.<br><br>Take Notes: MRI Primovist (Gd-EOB-DTPA) → most sensitive for CRC liver metastases | CT → excellent for staging, less sensitive for small lesions | PET-CT → whole-body staging, identifies extra-hepatic disease, detects recurrence | USS → first-line (cheap) but operator-dependent and misses small lesions | Decision for surgery → based on MRI FLR + ICG + general fitness",t:180},
  {q:"Carcinoid syndrome occurs when carcinoid tumours cause symptoms of flushing, diarrhoea, and bronchoconstriction. Why does carcinoid syndrome only occur with liver metastases (or primary tumours draining directly into systemic circulation)?",a:"Liver metastases produce more serotonin than primary gut tumours",b:"The portal circulation allows serotonin to pass directly to the systemic circulation without hepatic inactivation when the liver is bypassed by metastases",c:"Liver metastases express different receptors",d:"Gut primary tumours do not produce serotonin",ans:"B",e:"Serotonin (5-HT) from gut carcinoid tumours is normally inactivated by the liver (monoamine oxidase). When liver metastases exist, they drain directly into hepatic veins → systemic circulation, bypassing hepatic inactivation - allowing serotonin to reach the heart and lungs causing carcinoid syndrome.<br><br>Take Notes: Primary midgut carcinoid → portal drainage → serotonin inactivated in liver → no carcinoid syndrome | Liver metastases → systemic release → carcinoid syndrome | Appendix carcinoid → most common, rarely metastasises | Right heart disease → carcinoid heart disease (tricuspid + pulmonary valve lesions, from serotonin) | Treatment → somatostatin analogues (octreotide/lanreotide) + surgical debulking | Carcinoid crisis → octreotide IV",t:180},
  {q:"The MOST appropriate investigation for a patient with suspected acute mesenteric ischaemia (sudden severe abdominal pain out of proportion to physical findings, atrial fibrillation, leukocytosis 18,000, elevated lactate) is:",a:"Plain abdominal X-ray",b:"USS of the abdomen",c:"CT angiography of the mesenteric vessels (urgent)",d:"Diagnostic laparoscopy as first-line",ans:"C",e:"CT mesenteric angiography (CTMA) with IV contrast is the investigation of choice for acute mesenteric ischaemia - it can identify arterial occlusion (filling defect in SMA), venous thrombosis, pneumatosis intestinalis, and portal venous gas while also demonstrating bowel viability.<br><br>Take Notes: AMI → 'pain out of proportion to signs' + AF (embolic source) + high lactate | Causes → SMA embolism (50%, AF), SMA thrombosis (25%), mesenteric venous thrombosis (5-15%), non-occlusive (shock) | CT angiography → investigation of choice (also rules out other causes) | Treatment → immediate IV heparin + surgical embolectomy or catheter-directed thrombolysis | Peritonitis → emergency laparotomy | Mortality → 60-80% even with treatment (delays common)",t:180},
  {q:"A patient with Barrett's oesophagus (specialised intestinal metaplasia) undergoes surveillance endoscopy. Biopsy shows high-grade dysplasia (HGD) without invasive cancer. The most appropriate management is:",a:"Aggressive acid suppression with PPI and repeat endoscopy in 6 months",b:"Radiofrequency ablation (RFA) or endoscopic mucosal resection (EMR) - endoscopic eradication therapy",c:"Oesophagectomy as standard treatment for all HGD",d:"Watchful waiting with annual endoscopy",ans:"B",e:"HGD in Barrett's is now treated with endoscopic eradication therapy (radiofrequency ablation ± EMR of visible lesions) rather than oesophagectomy - RFA has 90%+ complete remission rates for HGD with lower morbidity. Oesophagectomy is reserved for true invasive cancer (T1b or deeper).<br><br>Take Notes: Barrett's → columnar metaplasia replacing squamous → risk of oesophageal adenocarcinoma | HGD → annual conversion to cancer ~8-10% | RFA → ablates metaplastic and dysplastic mucosa | EMR → for visible lesions/nodules (staging + treatment) | After RFA → metaplasia may recur → continued surveillance needed | Anti-reflux surgery does NOT reduce cancer risk significantly",t:180},
  {q:"In the ATLS primary survey, which finding is most immediately life-threatening and requires intervention FIRST before completing the survey?",a:"Hypotension (blood pressure 90/60 mmHg)",b:"Tension pneumothorax with absent breath sounds and tracheal deviation",c:"GCS 10",d:"Suspected femoral fracture",ans:"B",e:"Tension pneumothorax is immediately life-threatening - mediastinal shift compresses the contralateral lung and heart, causing cardiovascular collapse within minutes. It must be decompressed immediately (needle then chest drain) before any other assessment continues.<br><br>Take Notes: ATLS primary survey → ABCDE, life threats treated as found | Life threats → airway obstruction, tension pneumothorax, massive haemothorax, cardiac tamponade, open pneumothorax, flail chest | Tension pneumo → needle decompression 2nd ICS MCL then chest drain | Do NOT wait for CXR | Hypotension addressed during C (circulation) phase",t:180},
  {q:"A multiply-injured patient arrives with a GCS of 7, spontaneous breathing, and blood pressure 100/70 mmHg. After 2L IV crystalloid, BP improves to 120/80. Which action is most urgent?",a:"Immediate CT whole-body scan",b:"Definitive airway management (rapid sequence intubation) due to GCS ≤8",c:"Blood cultures and antibiotics",d:"Foley catheter insertion",ans:"B",e:"GCS ≤8 is the threshold for definitive airway management in trauma - patients cannot protect their airway and are at high risk of aspiration, hypoxia, and further neurological deterioration. RSI should occur before CT scanning.<br><br>Take Notes: GCS ≤8 → cannot protect airway → RSI required | MILS (manual in-line stabilisation) → during intubation if C-spine injury suspected | Airway secured BEFORE transfer to CT | 'Trauma cocktail' RSI → modified (fentanyl + ketamine + rocuronium) or suxamethonium (avoid if >24h post-burn/denervation) | Difficult airway in trauma → surgical airway if fails (cricothyroidotomy)",t:180},
  {q:"The MOST common solid organ injured in blunt abdominal trauma is:",a:"Liver",b:"Kidney",c:"Spleen",d:"Pancreas",ans:"C",e:"The spleen is the most commonly injured solid organ in blunt abdominal trauma - it is unprotected by the ribcage in its lower pole and is vulnerable to direct blows (left-sided). Liver injury is second most common and is associated with higher mortality due to the major hepatic veins.<br><br>Take Notes: Spleen → most common solid organ in blunt trauma | Liver → second most common, higher mortality | Pancreas → least common, very serious (ductal injury) | Kidney → retroperitoneal, relatively protected | Spleen grade I-V → grades I-III (low) → NOM in stable patients | Grade IV-V or unstable → splenectomy or SAE",t:180},
  {q:"A patient with a stab wound to the left 5th intercostal space presents with muffled heart sounds, distended neck veins, and hypotension (Beck's triad). After initial resuscitation, the next step is:",a:"CT scan to confirm haemopericardium",b:"Immediate pericardiocentesis or emergency thoracotomy",c:"IV fluids to increase preload",d:"Urgent echocardiography before any intervention",ans:"B",e:"Beck's triad (hypotension + distended neck veins + muffled heart sounds) indicates cardiac tamponade - immediate pericardiocentesis (subxiphoid approach) or emergency department thoracotomy (if arresting) is life-saving and should not be delayed for imaging.<br><br>Take Notes: Cardiac tamponade → Beck's triad + pulsus paradoxus (SBP falls >10 mmHg on inspiration) | Kussmaul sign → JVP rises on inspiration (also constrictive pericarditis) | FAST → pericardial fluid → sensitivity 90-95% for tamponade | Pericardiocentesis → blind subxiphoid or USS-guided | Definitive → surgical drainage (pericardial window or thoracotomy) | Traumatic → penetrating cardiac injury, highest mortality",t:180},
  {q:"A patient develops bilateral leg weakness 6 hours after blunt thoracic trauma. MRI shows a posterior cord contusion at T10. What neurological deficits would you expect?",a:"Loss of motor function + loss of pain and temperature sensation bilaterally below T10",b:"Loss of proprioception and vibration sense bilaterally below T10, with preserved pain/temperature and motor function",c:"Ipsilateral motor and proprioception loss with contralateral pain/temperature loss below T10",d:"Saddle anaesthesia and urinary incontinence only",ans:"B",e:"Posterior cord syndrome affects the dorsal columns (carrying proprioception, vibration, fine touch), which run posteriorly in the spinal cord. Pain and temperature (anterolateral spinothalamic tract) and motor function (anterolateral corticospinal tract) are preserved.<br><br>Take Notes: Posterior cord → dorsal columns → proprioception + vibration + fine touch impaired | Most rare cord syndrome | Anterior cord → motor + pain/temp impaired (dorsal columns preserved) | Brown-Sequard → hemisection (ipsilateral motor + proprioception loss, contralateral pain/temp) | Central cord → upper limbs > lower limbs + bladder | Conus medullaris → mixed UMN/LMN | Cauda equina → pure LMN",t:180},
  {q:"Management of an open femoral shaft fracture (Gustilo IIIB, contaminated wound) - the MOST appropriate temporary fixation while awaiting definitive management is:",a:"Skin traction",b:"External fixation with formal debridement",c:"Intramedullary nailing immediately",d:"Long leg plaster",ans:"B",e:"Gustilo IIIB open fractures require urgent washout and debridement (within 6-8 hours ideally) followed by temporary external fixation - this stabilises the fracture and allows soft tissue management before definitive internal fixation once wound contamination is controlled (usually 5-7 days).<br><br>Take Notes: Open fracture management → ABCDE → antibiotics (within 1h) → photograph wound → saline-soaked dressing → temporary external fixation | 'Fix and flap' → definitive fixation + soft tissue cover within 72h ideally | IIIC → also requires vascular repair | Intramedullary nailing → generally avoided acutely in contaminated IIIB/C (infection risk) | Wound culture → from debridement specimen",t:180},
  {q:"Traumatic aortic injury (TAI) most commonly occurs at the aortic isthmus because:",a:"The isthmus is the narrowest point of the aorta",b:"It is the junction between the mobile aortic arch and the fixed descending aorta, causing shearing forces during deceleration",c:"The isthmus lacks adventitial support",d:"The intercostal arteries weaken the aortic wall here",ans:"B",e:"During rapid deceleration (RTA, fall from height), the mobile arch continues forward while the descending aorta is fixed by the ligamentum arteriosum - the shearing force is concentrated at the aortic isthmus (ligamentum attachment), causing 90% of traumatic aortic injuries at this location.<br><br>Take Notes: Aortic isthmus → just distal to origin of left subclavian artery, at attachment of ligamentum arteriosum | 90% of traumatic aortic injuries → aortic isthmus | CXR → widened mediastinum + obliteration of aortic knuckle + left pleural effusion | CT angiography → definitive diagnosis | Treatment → TEVAR (endovascular) preferred over open repair | Permissive hypotension → target SBP 80-90 until aortic control",t:180},
  {q:"The Ottawa ankle rules help clinicians decide when to obtain X-rays after ankle injury. An X-ray is required if there is bony tenderness in the posterior half of the lateral or medial malleolus OR inability to weight-bear (4 steps) both immediately after and in the ED. The rule applies to patients:",a:"All patients with ankle injury",b:"Patients aged 18-55 years who are conscious and can cooperate",c:"Only patients with obvious deformity",d:"Only patients with medial malleolus tenderness",ans:"B",e:"Ottawa ankle rules apply to patients aged 18-55 who can cooperate and give reliable history - they are not validated in children, the elderly, intoxicated patients, or those with diminished sensation. They have 96-98% sensitivity for fractures but only 30-40% specificity (many unnecessary X-rays).<br><br>Take Notes: Ottawa ankle rules → X-ray if: bone tenderness at posterior 6cm of fibula/lateral malleolus OR posterior 6cm of tibia/medial malleolus OR base of 5th metatarsal OR navicular AND/OR inability to weight bear 4 steps | Sensitivity 96-98% → if negative, unlikely fracture | Ottawa foot rules → also include midfoot | Not validated → children <18, elderly, intoxicated, insensate",t:180},
  {q:"Crush syndrome (traumatic rhabdomyolysis) after prolonged limb compression causes acute kidney injury through which mechanism?",a:"Direct compression of renal vessels",b:"Myoglobin tubular precipitation in acidic, concentrated urine causing tubular obstruction and direct toxicity",c:"Immune complex deposition in the glomeruli",d:"Hypotension-mediated cortical ischaemia only",ans:"B",e:"Released myoglobin from crushed muscle precipitates in the renal tubules (especially distal tubule) in acidic urine, forming tubular casts that obstruct flow - myoglobin is also directly toxic to tubular cells via free radical generation and vasoconstriction of afferent arterioles.<br><br>Take Notes: Rhabdomyolysis → CK >5000 IU/L + myoglobinuria (tea/cola-coloured urine) | Treatment → aggressive IV fluids (saline, target UO >200 mL/h) + urinary alkalinisation (sodium bicarbonate) + avoid nephrotoxins | ECG → hyperkalaemia from massive K+ release from necrotic muscle | 'K+ from the dead' → dangerous hyperkalaemia | Compartment syndrome → can cause rhabdomyolysis even without external crush",t:180},
  {q:"A patient with acute cholecystitis undergoes laparoscopic cholecystectomy. During dissection in the Calot triangle, the surgeon inadvertently divides a structure that results in immediate bile flooding of the operative field. The most likely injury is:",a:"Injury to the cystic duct",b:"Common bile duct injury (most serious and most feared biliary complication)",c:"Injury to the right hepatic artery",d:"Injury to the cystic artery",ans:"B",e:"CBD injury is the most serious complication of laparoscopic cholecystectomy - occurring in 0.3-0.5% vs 0.1-0.2% of open cholecystectomies. The most common type is misidentification of CBD as cystic duct and clipping/dividing it. Prevention: critical view of safety before clipping.<br><br>Take Notes: Critical view of safety (Strasberg) → two structures entering GB neck + lower third of cystic plate exposed | Intraoperative cholangiogram → if anatomy unclear | Bile duct injury management → immediate recognition + biliary reconstruction (Roux-en-Y hepaticojejunostomy) at specialist centre | Delayed recognition → biliary peritonitis + stricture | Mortality → bile duct injury increases overall mortality 5-fold",t:180},
  {q:"The most important prognostic factor in colorectal cancer is:",a:"Tumour differentiation (grade)",b:"CEA level at diagnosis",c:"TNM stage, particularly lymph node involvement",d:"Tumour size",ans:"C",e:"TNM stage (particularly N status - lymph node involvement) is the most powerful independent prognostic factor in CRC - N0 disease has 5-year survival >80%, while N2 (≥4 nodes) is ~35%. Lymph node involvement determines need for adjuvant chemotherapy.<br><br>Take Notes: CRC staging → Stage I (T1-2, N0) 90% 5yr; Stage II (T3-4, N0) 70-80%; Stage III (any T, N+) 35-65%; Stage IV (M1) 5-10% | Adjuvant chemo → Stage III (FOLFOX) + high-risk Stage II | MSI (microsatellite instability) → better prognosis in Stage II, predicts poor response to 5-FU, responds to immunotherapy | Lynch syndrome → MMR deficiency → MSI-H",t:180},
  {q:"A 60-year-old patient presents with progressive dysphagia (solids first, then liquids), weight loss, and a CT showing a circumferential oesophageal tumour at the gastro-oesophageal junction. The most common histological type at the GOJ in the Western world is:",a:"Squamous cell carcinoma",b:"Adenocarcinoma",c:"Leiomyosarcoma",d:"Lymphoma",ans:"B",e:"Adenocarcinoma now accounts for >50% of oesophageal cancers in Western countries (related to GORD and Barrett's metaplasia) and dominates the lower oesophagus/GOJ. Squamous cell carcinoma is still more common worldwide (upper/mid oesophagus, related to smoking and alcohol).<br><br>Take Notes: Western countries → adenocarcinoma (lower oesophagus/GOJ, Barrett's related) | Developing countries → SCC dominates (upper/mid, smoking + alcohol + hot beverages + nutritional deficiency) | Staging → EUS (T and N) + CT + PET-CT | Siewert classification → Type I (GOJ adenocarcinoma), Type II (cardia), Type III (subcardial stomach) | Treatment → neoadjuvant FLOT (adenocarcinoma) or CROSS (SCC) then surgery",t:180},
  {q:"A patient who underwent a right hemicolectomy is found on post-operative day 3 to have a serum albumin of 18 g/L. Which nutritional intervention should be considered?",a:"Oral protein supplements only",b:"High-protein enteral nutrition via nasogastric tube",c:"Continue normal fluids until bowel sounds return",d:"Parenteral nutrition as albumin <25 g/L is an absolute indicator",ans:"B",e:"Serum albumin <25 g/L indicates severe malnutrition - early enteral nutrition via NGT is preferred over TPN (lower complication rate, maintains gut integrity). Enteral nutrition should begin early (12-24h post-operatively) rather than waiting for flatus.<br><br>Take Notes: Albumin → delayed nutritional indicator (t½ 20 days), not accurate for acute nutritional status | Pre-albumin/transthyretin → better acute marker (t½ 2-3 days) | Malnutrition screening → MUST score (Malnutrition Universal Screening Tool) | Enteral > parenteral always unless gut non-functional | NICE → offer oral nutritional support first, then enteral, then parenteral as last resort",t:180},
  {q:"A 65-year-old male with chronic hepatitis C-related cirrhosis (Child-Pugh B) develops a 3 cm hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC). AFP is 850 ng/mL. He has no extrahepatic disease and preserved liver function. According to the Barcelona Clinic Liver Cancer (BCLC) staging, the most appropriate treatment is:",a:"Sorafenib (systemic therapy only)",b:"Percutaneous radiofrequency ablation (RFA) for early stage HCC (BCLC 0/A)",c:"TACE (transarterial chemoembolisation) as first-line for 3 cm lesion",d:"Liver transplantation within Milan criteria as definitive treatment",ans:"D",e:"Milan criteria for liver transplantation: single HCC ≤5 cm OR ≤3 nodules all ≤3 cm, no vascular invasion, no extrahepatic disease - this patient (single 3 cm, no extrahepatic disease, cirrhotic Child-Pugh B) is within Milan criteria, making transplant the most appropriate curative option.<br><br>Take Notes: BCLC 0 (very early) → single <2 cm → ablation | BCLC A (early) → single or up to 3 ≤3 cm + compensated → resection OR ablation OR transplant | BCLC B (intermediate) → multifocal, no extrahepatic → TACE | BCLC C (advanced) → portal invasion/extrahepatic → sorafenib/atezolizumab-bevacizumab | Milan criteria → liver transplant standard | Beyond Milan → UCSF or downstaging with TACE",t:180},
  {q:"The Hinchey classification is used for perforated diverticular disease. Hinchey Stage III indicates:",a:"Localised pericolic abscess",b:"Distant abscess (pelvic, inter-loop)",c:"Purulent peritonitis from ruptured pericolic or pelvic abscess",d:"Faecal peritonitis from free perforation with faecal contamination",ans:"C",e:"Hinchey III is purulent (pus) peritonitis where a diverticular abscess has ruptured into the peritoneal cavity - the pus does not contain faecal material. Treatment options include laparoscopic washout and drain vs Hartmann's procedure.<br><br>Take Notes: Hinchey I → localised pericolic abscess | Hinchey II → distant (pelvic) abscess | Hinchey III → purulent peritonitis | Hinchey IV → faecal peritonitis (free perforation) | Hinchey I/II → CT-guided drainage ± antibiotics | Hinchey III → laparoscopic washout (LADIES trial shows acceptable) OR Hartmann's | Hinchey IV → Hartmann's or on-table washout + primary anastomosis + defunctioning stoma",t:180},
  {q:"A 45-year-old woman with Crohn's disease involving the terminal ileum for 15 years presents with right iliac fossa pain and an inflammatory mass. CT shows a 5 cm abscess adjacent to the terminal ileum. The next step is:",a:"Immediate ileocaecal resection",b:"Urgent colonoscopy",c:"CT-guided percutaneous drainage + IV antibiotics, then elective resection",d:"High-dose IV steroids to reduce inflammation",ans:"C",e:"Abscess from Crohn's disease should be drained percutaneously (when accessible on CT) and treated with antibiotics first - this settles the acute sepsis, allowing elective resection in an optimal nutritional and inflammatory state with much lower perioperative risk than emergency surgery.<br><br>Take Notes: Crohn abscess → percutaneous drainage + antibiotics + optimisation (nutrition, steroids weaned, biologics stopped) → then elective resection | Biologics (infliximab, adalimumab) → must be stopped 4-6 weeks pre-surgery (infection risk + poor healing) | Aim → single definitive operation in clean field | Emergency resection → only for perforation, obstruction not responding to treatment, haemorrhage | Strictureplasty → for short segments, preserves bowel",t:180},
  {q:"A 35-year-old footballer twists his knee and has immediate effusion (haemarthrosis) developing within 2 hours. The most common cause of acute traumatic haemarthrosis of the knee is:",a:"Medial collateral ligament rupture",b:"Anterior cruciate ligament tear (ACL rupture in ~70% of acute traumatic haemarthrosis)",c:"Meniscal tear only",d:"Posterior capsule injury",ans:"B",e:"ACL rupture is the most common cause of acute traumatic haemarthrosis (blood within knee joint) - the ACL is very vascular and bleeding is immediate. Haemarthrosis within 2 hours of injury is the single most important clinical predictor of significant intra-articular injury.<br><br>Take Notes: Haemarthrosis within 2h → 70% probability of ACL tear | Haemarthrosis within 2-24h → higher probability of meniscal tear (gradual synovial bleeding) | MCL → extraarticular, unlikely to cause haemarthrosis | Investigation → MRI knee (gold standard) | ACL tear management → physiotherapy vs reconstruction depending on instability + activity level",t:180},
  {q:"The treatment of choice for a displaced scaphoid fracture of the waist in a young active adult is:",a:"Below-elbow plaster cast for 12 weeks",b:"Internal fixation with a headless compression screw (Herbert screw)",c:"Above-elbow plaster for 6 weeks only",d:"Immobilisation in wrist flexion",ans:"B",e:"Displaced scaphoid fractures have high rates of non-union and AVN with conservative management - percutaneous or open internal fixation with a headless compression screw (Herbert type) allows early mobilisation and achieves 90-95% union rate, significantly better than cast immobilisation for displaced fractures.<br><br>Take Notes: Non-union risk → proximal pole fractures (blood supply enters distally), displacement, delayed treatment | Scaphoid blood supply → enters distally → proximal pole has retrograde supply (highest AVN risk) | Displaced (>1 mm) → surgical fixation | Undisplaced → can cast (6-12 weeks) but surgery equally valid | AVN → seen on MRI as low signal on T1 in proximal pole",t:180},
  {q:"Osgood-Schlatter disease presents in active adolescents with anterior knee pain localised to the tibial tuberosity. The underlying pathology is:",a:"Stress fracture of the tibial plateau",b:"Traction apophysitis of the tibial tubercle from repetitive quadriceps pull",c:"Osteochondritis dissecans of the lateral femoral condyle",d:"Prepatellar bursitis",ans:"B",e:"Osgood-Schlatter is a traction apophysitis - repetitive quadriceps contraction via the patellar tendon avulses small fragments from the incompletely ossified tibial apophysis during the adolescent growth spurt, causing pain, swelling, and a prominent tibial tubercle.<br><br>Take Notes: Age → 10-15 years, boys > girls | Activity-related pain at tibial tuberosity | Treatment → activity modification + analgesia + stretching (self-limiting) | Rarely → surgical excision of ossicle in adults with persistent symptoms | Sinding-Larsen-Johansson → similar but at inferior patellar pole | Distinguish from patellar tendon rupture → older patients, acute, loss of extension",t:180},
  {q:"A 70-year-old woman with osteoporosis has sustained a T12 vertebral compression fracture after minimal trauma. She has severe pain but no neurological deficit. Conservative management has failed after 6 weeks. The most appropriate minimally invasive treatment is:",a:"Open decompression and fusion",b:"Vertebroplasty or kyphoplasty (percutaneous cement augmentation)",c:"Long-term bed rest and opioid analgesia",d:"External bracing for 6 months",ans:"B",e:"Percutaneous vertebroplasty (cement injection into fractured vertebra) or balloon kyphoplasty (balloon expansion to restore height then cement) provides rapid pain relief (within 24-48 hours) in osteoporotic vertebral compression fractures with no neurological deficit that fail conservative management.<br><br>Take Notes: Vertebroplasty → cement injection only (may restore strength, no height restoration) | Kyphoplasty → balloon first (partial height restoration, lower cement leak risk) | Both → significant pain reduction in 85-90% | Risks → cement extravasation (1-10%), adjacent level fracture | Indications → persistent severe pain + no neurological deficit + failure of conservative management (≥4-6 weeks) | Bisphosphonates + calcium + vitamin D → prevent further fractures",t:180},
  {q:"The standard treatment for a displaced intracapsular fracture of the femoral neck in a physiologically FIT 75-year-old patient with prior mobility limitation (housebound, using stick) is:",a:"Total hip replacement",b:"Hemiarthroplasty (cemented unipolar or bipolar)",c:"Dynamic hip screw fixation",d:"Conservative management",ans:"B",e:"Hemiarthroplasty is the standard for displaced intracapsular neck of femur fractures in elderly patients with limited mobility and cognitive impairment - total hip replacement is preferred for active, physiologically fit patients with good mobility, as it has better functional outcomes but requires more careful patient selection.<br><br>Take Notes: Displaced intracapsular NOF → high AVN risk (>30%) → do NOT fix, replace | Active elderly (independent community ambulator) → THR (better function, more durable) | Less active/housebound → cemented hemiarthroplasty (Exeter or Thompson stem) | NICE guideline → THA for mobile patients without cognitive impairment | Cemented → lower periprosthetic fracture risk in osteoporotic bone | Fast-track hip → enhanced recovery protocol",t:180},
  {q:"The most reliable radiological sign of osteomyelitis on plain X-ray is:",a:"Soft tissue swelling (appears at 1-3 days)",b:"Osteopaenia at the site of infection",c:"Periosteal new bone formation (appears at 7-14 days)",d:"Loss of medullary trabeculation",ans:"C",e:"Periosteal new bone formation (periosteal reaction) is the most reliable sign of osteomyelitis on plain X-ray - it represents subperiosteal new bone formed in response to infection elevating the periosteum. However, it only appears at 7-14 days, making early diagnosis radiographically challenging.<br><br>Take Notes: Plain X-ray changes → soft tissue oedema (1-3d) → osteopaenia (7-14d) → periosteal reaction (7-14d) → cortical destruction (10-21d) | MRI → detects within 24-48h of onset (gold standard for early detection) | Bone scan → sensitive but non-specific | USS → subperiosteal collection (guides aspiration) | Gold standard for microbiological diagnosis → bone biopsy/aspiration culture",t:180},
  {q:"A 55-year-old male develops sudden onset back pain after lifting a heavy object. MRI shows a large L4/L5 disc prolapse compressing the S1 nerve root. Which of the following features would indicate urgent surgical intervention?",a:"Pain radiating to the lateral foot and heel",b:"Absent ankle jerk",c:"Cauda equina syndrome with urinary retention and saddle anaesthesia",d:"Weakness of plantar flexion (S1)",ans:"C",e:"Cauda equina syndrome (urinary retention/incontinence + saddle anaesthesia + bilateral leg weakness) is a surgical emergency requiring urgent MRI and decompression within 48 hours - delay leads to permanent incontinence and neurological deficit.<br><br>Take Notes: L4/L5 disc → commonly compresses L5 nerve root (foot drop) | L5/S1 disc → commonly compresses S1 (absent ankle jerk, weak plantar flexion) | Cauda equina syndrome → MRI within 4h, surgery within 48h (ideally within 24h) | Failed conservative management at 6 weeks → consider surgery | Relative indications → progressive neurological deficit, severe pain not responding to analgesia",t:180},
  {q:"A patient sustains a Monteggia fracture-dislocation. The classic pattern of injury is:",a:"Fracture of the distal radius with disruption of the distal radio-ulnar joint",b:"Fracture of the proximal third of the ulna with anterior (most common) dislocation of the radial head",c:"Fracture of the distal ulna with dislocation of the radiocarpal joint",d:"Fracture of both radius and ulna with posterior displacement",ans:"B",e:"Monteggia fracture-dislocation = fracture of the proximal ulnar shaft + radial head dislocation. The Bado classification describes four types based on the direction of radial head dislocation - Type I (anterior, 60-70%) is most common and involves hyperflexion mechanism.<br><br>Take Notes: Monteggia → ulna shaft fracture + radial head dislocation (any radius-ulna fracture requires checking the proximal AND distal joint) | Galeazzi → distal radius fracture + DRUJ disruption | Bado I → anterior radial head dislocation (most common, extension injury) | Adult Monteggia → open reduction essential (closed reduction rarely holds) | PIN (posterior interosseous nerve) → at risk from radial head dislocation",t:180},
  {q:"Cerebral autoregulation maintains constant cerebral blood flow despite changes in MAP between which range?",a:"40-80 mmHg",b:"50-150 mmHg",c:"60-200 mmHg",d:"70-130 mmHg",ans:"B",e:"Between MAP 50-150 mmHg, cerebrovascular autoregulation maintains CBF at ~50 mL/100g/min by adjusting cerebrovascular resistance. Below 50 mmHg, CBF falls passively (ischaemia risk). Above 150 mmHg, breakthrough vasodilation causes hyperaemia and cerebral oedema.<br><br>Take Notes: Autoregulation maintained → MAP 50-150 mmHg | Chronic hypertension → shifts autoregulation curve rightward (tolerates higher MAP, more sensitive to hypotension) | Loss of autoregulation → traumatic brain injury, SAH → passive CBF-pressure relationship | CPP = MAP - ICP | Target CPP in TBI → >60 mmHg | CO2 → most potent regulator (overrides autoregulation)",t:180},
  {q:"Which finding on a CT head after traumatic brain injury represents the MOST immediate surgical emergency?",a:"Cerebral contusion with surrounding oedema",b:"Diffuse axonal injury (DAI)",c:"Acute subdural haematoma with midline shift >5 mm and patient deteriorating",d:"Subarachnoid haemorrhage without hydrocephalus",ans:"C",e:"Acute SDH with midline shift >5 mm in a deteriorating patient represents transtentorial herniation - emergency craniotomy and evacuation within 4 hours of injury gives the best neurological outcome. Mortality rises dramatically with delay in decompression.<br><br>Take Notes: Acute SDH → venous (bridging veins), crescent-shaped | Indications for surgery → thickness >10mm OR midline shift >5mm OR Glasgow Coma Scale drop ≥2 | Outcome worse than EDH (underlying brain injury) | EDH → arterial (MMA), biconvex → more favourable if evacuated promptly | DAI → diffuse white matter axonal injury, MRI diagnosis, no surgical treatment | 'Talk and die' → EDH (lucid interval then deterioration)",t:180},
  {q:"The MOST common cause of hydrocephalus in adults is:",a:"Congenital aqueduct stenosis",b:"Obstruction of CSF flow by tumours, haemorrhage, or meningitis (non-communicating) or impaired absorption (communicating)",c:"Meningitis in childhood",d:"Choroid plexus papilloma",ans:"B",e:"Adult hydrocephalus is most commonly acquired - obstruction by tumours/haemorrhage (non-communicating/obstructive) or impaired CSF absorption at the arachnoid granulations after subarachnoid haemorrhage, meningitis, or trauma (communicating).<br><br>Take Notes: Non-communicating → intraventricular obstruction (aqueduct, foramina of Luschka/Magendie) | Communicating → impaired arachnoid granule reabsorption | Normal pressure hydrocephalus (NPH) → communicating, idiopathic | Treatment → VP shunt or endoscopic third ventriculostomy (ETV for non-communicating) | ETV → creates communication between third ventricle and subarachnoid space | Complications → shunt infection (S. epidermidis most common) and overdrainage",t:180},
  {q:"Anterior cervical discectomy and fusion (ACDF) for C5-C6 disc herniation with C6 radiculopathy - which post-operative complication requires immediate re-exploration?",a:"Dysphagia (expected, usually resolves)",b:"Persistent C6 distribution paraesthesia",c:"Expanding cervical haematoma causing airway compression",d:"Hoarseness from vocal cord oedema",ans:"C",e:"Post-ACDF haematoma expanding in the confined cervical space can cause life-threatening airway compression within minutes - it requires immediate wound exploration at the bedside (or theatre) to release the haematoma and re-intubate before complete airway obstruction occurs.<br><br>Take Notes: ACDF complications → dysphagia (30-50%, usually temporary), recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy, Horner syndrome, adjacent segment disease | Immediate threat → expanding haematoma (1-2%) | Management → open wound immediately if airway deteriorating (bedside decompression) | Recurrent laryngeal nerve → more common on left approach | C-spine surgery → check neurological status in recovery room",t:180},
  {q:"A 28-year-old professional boxer develops headaches and cognitive decline 5 years after retiring. CT head shows diffuse cerebral atrophy and cavum septum pellucidum. This is consistent with:",a:"Normal pressure hydrocephalus",b:"Chronic traumatic encephalopathy (CTE)",c:"Alzheimer's disease",d:"Frontotemporal dementia",ans:"B",e:"Chronic traumatic encephalopathy (CTE) - a neurodegenerative condition caused by repetitive brain trauma - is characterised by progressive cognitive decline, behavioural changes, and ultimately dementia. Cavum septum pellucidum is a characteristic radiological finding (expansion of the cavity in the septum pellucidum from repeated concussions).<br><br>Take Notes: CTE → found in NFL players, boxers, rugby players, war veterans with blast injuries | Pathology → tau protein accumulation (NFTs in perivascular regions) | Diagnosis → confirmed only at post-mortem (tau immunohistochemistry) | Cavum septum pellucidum → ~80% of boxers (repetitive trauma to the temporal horns) | Prevention → limit head impacts + early retirement after concussion | Management → symptomatic only (no disease-modifying treatment)",t:180},
  {q:"A 55-year-old woman develops sudden-onset severe headache (thunderclap) with meningism. CT head is normal. LP shows xanthochromic CSF. The next investigation to identify the cause is:",a:"MRI brain with gadolinium",b:"CT angiography of the cerebral vessels (or digital subtraction angiography)",c:"EEG",d:"SPECT scan",ans:"B",e:"After confirming SAH (CT ± LP), CT angiography identifies the causative aneurysm in >90% of cases and guides treatment planning (endovascular coiling vs surgical clipping). DSA (catheter angiography) remains gold standard if CTA is non-diagnostic.<br><br>Take Notes: SAH confirmed → CTA to identify aneurysm | CTA sensitivity → 95-99% for aneurysms >3mm | DSA → gold standard, also therapeutic (coiling) | AComA → most common aneurysm site | Perimesencephalic SAH → pattern suggesting venous cause, benign prognosis | Treatment timing → within 24-72h to prevent re-bleeding (early intervention reduces vasospasm window) | Vasospasm → peaks day 7-10, causes delayed cerebral ischaemia",t:180},
  {q:"Raised intracranial pressure (ICP) - the Cushing response (Cushing's triad) is a sign of impending brainstem herniation. It consists of:",a:"Hypotension + tachycardia + irregular respiration",b:"Hypertension (widened pulse pressure) + bradycardia + irregular respiration",c:"Hypertension + tachycardia + apnoea",d:"Bradycardia + hypotension + hyperventilation",ans:"B",e:"The Cushing response is a late brainstem ischaemia reflex: rising ICP → brainstem ischaemia → sympathetic surge → systolic hypertension (widened pulse pressure) → baroreceptor-mediated vagal bradycardia → irregular/Cheyne-Stokes respiration. It signifies impending cerebral herniation.<br><br>Take Notes: Cushing reflex → hypertension (raised SBP, wide pulse pressure) + bradycardia (vagal) + irregular respirations | Late sign of raised ICP → herniation imminent | Management → urgent CT + neurosurgical consultation | ICP lowering → head up 30°, controlled hyperventilation (temporising), mannitol/hypertonic saline, sedation, surgical decompression | Not to be confused with Cushing syndrome (from cortisol excess)",t:180},
  {q:"Stereotactic radiosurgery (Gamma Knife) is MOST appropriate for:",a:"Large infiltrating glioblastoma multiforme",b:"Acoustic neuroma (vestibular schwannoma) <3 cm in diameter in a patient unfit for surgery",c:"Any brain tumour requiring immediate cytoreduction",d:"Acute intracranial haemorrhage",ans:"B",e:"Stereotactic radiosurgery delivers a precise high dose of radiation to a defined small target (single session) - it is most effective for well-circumscribed, small, non-infiltrating lesions such as acoustic neuromas <3 cm, brain metastases, and cavernous malformations. It cannot treat large infiltrating tumours.<br><br>Take Notes: Stereotactic radiosurgery → highly focused single-dose irradiation (Gamma Knife, CyberKnife, LINAC-based) | Indications → acoustic neuroma, brain metastases (1-3 lesions, <3 cm), meningioma, AVM, trigeminal neuralgia | Cannot treat → large tumours, diffuse gliomas, acute haemorrhage | Local control → 90-95% for acoustic neuromas | Hearing preservation → better than microsurgery in suitable cases",t:180},
  {q:"The PSA (prostate-specific antigen) density is calculated as PSA/prostate volume. A PSA density of >0.15 ng/mL/cc is associated with increased risk of clinically significant prostate cancer. PSA is NOT cancer-specific - it is elevated in:",a:"Prostate cancer only",b:"BPH, prostatitis, ejaculation, urinary catheterisation, DRE, and prostate cancer",c:"All urinary tract conditions",d:"Prostate cancer and bladder cancer equally",ans:"B",e:"PSA is organ-specific but NOT cancer-specific - BPH, prostatitis, DRE, urinary catheterisation, cystoscopy, prostatic biopsy, and ejaculation all elevate PSA. This leads to a high false-positive rate (70-80% of men with PSA 4-10 ng/mL have benign conditions).<br><br>Take Notes: PSA density → helps distinguish cancer from BPH | PSA velocity → rising PSA over time (suspicious if >0.75 ng/mL/yr) | Free:total PSA ratio → lower ratio = higher cancer risk | PSA >4 → biopsy (traditionally) | MRI-TRUS fusion biopsy → improved detection of significant cancers | Active surveillance → for low-risk prostate cancer (PSA <10, Gleason 3+3, T1-T2a, ≤2 cores positive)",t:180},
  {q:"The treatment of nephrotic syndrome from minimal change disease in an adult differs from children because:",a:"Adults should not receive steroids",b:"Adults require renal biopsy before treatment (diagnosis cannot be assumed)",c:"Adults always progress to renal failure",d:"Children do not respond to steroids",ans:"B",e:"In children with nephrotic syndrome (<10 years), minimal change disease is assumed without biopsy and empirical steroid therapy is started - 90% respond. In adults, nephrotic syndrome has a wide differential (MCD, FSGS, membranous, diabetic, amyloid) requiring biopsy for diagnosis before treatment.<br><br>Take Notes: Minimal change disease → most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children (90%) | Adults → biopsy required (MCD only 15-25% of adult nephrotic syndrome) | MCD → podocyte effacement on EM (no immune deposits) | Treatment → prednisolone 1 mg/kg/day, most adults respond but more likely to relapse | Non-responders → cyclophosphamide or ciclosporin | FSGS → frequent MCD non-responder",t:180},
  {q:"A 50-year-old male has a PSA of 24 ng/mL. Transrectal MRI-guided biopsy shows Gleason score 4+3=7 (Grade Group 3), T2cN0M0. This represents:",a:"Low-risk prostate cancer suitable for active surveillance",b:"High-risk localised prostate cancer",c:"Intermediate-risk disease",d:"Very high-risk disease",ans:"B",e:"Intermediate-risk prostate cancer (NICE/EAU): PSA 10-20 ng/mL OR Gleason 7 (3+4 or 4+3) OR T2b-c disease. This patient has a borderline PSA (24, slightly above 20) and Grade Group 3 (4+3 = unfavourable intermediate risk) - this is intermediate to high risk, but Gleason 4+3 classifies as unfavourable intermediate.<br><br>Take Notes: Low-risk → PSA <10 + Gleason ≤6 (GG1) + T1-T2a | Intermediate → PSA 10-20 OR Gleason 7 OR T2b-c | High-risk → PSA >20 OR Gleason ≥8 (GG4-5) OR T3-T4 | PSA 24 = borderline high-risk | Gleason 4+3 = GG3 (unfavourable intermediate) | Treatment intermediate-risk → radical prostatectomy OR radical RT ± short-term ADT",t:180},
  {q:"The PCNL (percutaneous nephrolithotomy) approach to the kidney requires placement of a working sheath. The optimal access calyx for a staghorn calculus is usually:",a:"Upper pole calyx (subcostal approach)",b:"Lower pole calyx (provides best access but poor for upper pole stones)",c:"Middle calyx",d:"Posterior calyx of the middle pole or upper pole depending on stone anatomy",ans:"D",e:"For staghorn calculi, the optimal access is usually through a posterior upper or middle calyx under fluoroscopic/USS guidance - upper pole access (intercostal approach) provides best access to the renal pelvis and all calyces. Multiple punctures may be needed for complex staghorn stones.<br><br>Take Notes: PCNL → for renal stones >2 cm or ESWL failures or lower pole stones >1 cm | Upper pole access → best for staghorn (extends through upper, middle, lower pole) | Risk → intercostal access risks pleura → pneumothorax | Lower pole puncture → best for lower pole stones only | Prone position → standard for PCNL | Complication rate → bleeding (~5%), infection, injury to adjacent organs | Clearance rate → 80-90% for staghorn (multiple sessions may be needed)",t:180},
  {q:"A 65-year-old male has an incidentally found 15 cm adrenal mass on CT abdomen. Biochemical investigations show elevated urinary free cortisol, DHEAS, and androgens. The next step is:",a:"Repeat CT in 6 months",b:"MRI adrenal for characterisation",c:"Adrenalectomy (suspicious for adrenocortical carcinoma - size + hormonal activity)",d:"CT-guided biopsy",ans:"C",e:"ACC is strongly suspected with a large adrenal mass (>4 cm, especially >6 cm) combined with hormonal activity - biopsy is contraindicated (risks seeding, phaeochromocytoma must first be excluded) and surgery is indicated. ACC is the most likely diagnosis here, carrying poor prognosis.<br><br>Take Notes: ACC → rare, aggressive, size >6 cm + hormonal activity (cortisol ± androgens ± oestrogens) | Biopsy contraindicated → until phaeochromocytoma excluded (risk of hypertensive crisis) + risk of peritoneal seeding | Open adrenalectomy preferred → for ACC to achieve R0 resection | Laparoscopic → only for benign lesions | Cushing syndrome from ACC → often incomplete (mixed hormone production) | Mitotane → adjuvant chemotherapy for ACC | Median survival → 18-36 months",t:180},
  {q:"Retrograde ejaculation (ejaculating into the bladder) is a recognised complication of which procedure?",a:"TURBT (transurethral resection of bladder tumour)",b:"Transurethral resection of the prostate (TURP)",c:"Radical prostatectomy",d:"Hydrocele repair",ans:"B",e:"TURP disrupts the bladder neck mechanism (internal sphincter, sympathetically controlled - prevents retrograde ejaculation) in ~65-90% of men. Retrograde ejaculation allows normal orgasm but infertility - this should be counselled pre-operatively as a common permanent effect of TURP.<br><br>Take Notes: TURP complications → retrograde ejaculation (65-90%), TUR syndrome (glycine absorption, dilutional hyponatraemia), bleeding, urethral stricture | TUR syndrome → classic presentation: confusion + hyponatraemia after prolonged TURP (glycine irrigation absorbed) | Retrograde ejaculation → post-void urine has sperm (diagnosis) | Radical prostatectomy → erectile dysfunction + urinary incontinence (different complications) | Holmium laser (HoLEP) → reduced TUR syndrome risk",t:180},
  {q:"A child presents with a non-tender scrotal mass that transilluminates brilliantly and does not reduce. USS shows a lesion separate from the testis. The most likely diagnosis is:",a:"Hydrocele of the tunica vaginalis",b:"Inguinal hernia extending into the scrotum",c:"Epididymal cyst",d:"Testicular teratoma",ans:"C",e:"An epididymal cyst (spermatocele) is a smooth, spherical, brilliantly transilluminant swelling that lies SEPARATE from the testis (felt distinctly above or behind the testis) - unlike a hydrocele which surrounds the testis and cannot be felt separately from it.<br><br>Take Notes: Epididymal cyst → separate from testis, transilluminant, benign | Hydrocele → surrounds testis, cannot 'get above' the swelling, transilluminant | Testicular tumour → solid, non-transilluminant, cannot get above it | Varicocele → bag of worms, left-sided, non-transilluminant | USS → distinguishes solid from cystic, confirms testicular vs epididymal | Epididymal cyst → no treatment needed unless symptomatic",t:180},
  {q:"The gold standard for diagnosis of constrictive pericarditis (showing ventricular interdependence) is:",a:"CT thorax showing pericardial thickening",b:"Cardiac catheterisation demonstrating equalisation of diastolic pressures (RVEDP = LVEDP = PCWP) and square root sign on ventricular pressure trace",c:"Echocardiography showing pericardial effusion",d:"MRI showing pericardial inflammation",ans:"B",e:"Cardiac catheterisation in constrictive pericarditis shows the pathognomonic pattern: equalisation of right and left heart diastolic pressures (RVEDP = LVEDP = PCWP, all 15-25 mmHg) and the 'square root sign' (dip-plateau pattern) on ventricular pressure traces from the rigid pericardium constraining diastolic filling.<br><br>Take Notes: Constrictive pericarditis → fibrous rigid pericardium limits cardiac filling | Features → raised JVP + Kussmaul sign (JVP rises on inspiration) + pericardial knock (early diastolic) | CT → pericardial thickening (>4mm) + calcification | MRI → pericardial fibrosis (dark on both T1 and T2) | Cardiac cath → equalised diastolic pressures + dip-plateau pattern | Treatment → pericardiectomy (stripping) | Distinguishing from restrictive CMP → complex, MRI + cath needed",t:180},
  {q:"After coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG), the LEFT internal mammary artery (LIMA) is preferred for grafting the LAD because:",a:"It is longer than saphenous vein and easier to use",b:"It has 90-95% patency at 10 years vs 50-60% for SVG at 10 years, significantly reducing re-operation rates and improving survival",c:"It is technically easier to harvest than the saphenous vein",d:"It has less chance of spasm than other conduits",ans:"B",e:"LIMA to LAD has the best long-term patency of any coronary conduit - 90-95% at 10 years compared to 50-60% for saphenous vein grafts. This superior patency translates into improved 10-year survival benefit and reduction in re-operation rates, making LIMA-LAD the cornerstone of CABG.<br><br>Take Notes: LIMA-LAD → gold standard CABG conduit | Other arterial conduits → RIMA, radial artery (90%+ at 5 years) | SVG patency → 80% at 1 year, 60% at 5 years, 50% at 10 years (intimal hyperplasia + atherosclerosis) | Total arterial revascularisation → bilateral IMA + radial for multiple grafts | IMA → pedicled (leaves periosteum + fascia intact) or skeletonised (longer graft) | IMA patency → related to competitive flow (LAD stenosis must be >70%)",t:180},
  {q:"Cardiac tamponade differs from constrictive pericarditis in that cardiac tamponade:",a:"Causes Kussmaul sign (JVP rises on inspiration)",b:"Causes pulsus paradoxus (SBP falls >10 mmHg on inspiration) and is usually more acute",c:"Shows equalisation of diastolic pressures on cardiac catheterisation",d:"Has prominent Y descent on JVP waveform",ans:"B",e:"Cardiac tamponade causes pulsus paradoxus - on inspiration, right ventricular filling increases (venous return), but in the rigid pericardium this compresses the LV, reducing LV filling and output, dropping SBP by >10 mmHg. Constrictive pericarditis shows Kussmaul sign instead.<br><br>Take Notes: Tamponade → pulsus paradoxus + raised JVP + hypotension + muffled heart sounds (Beck's triad) + absent Kussmaul | Constrictive pericarditis → Kussmaul sign + pericardial knock + no pulsus paradoxus | JVP waveform → tamponade: absent Y descent (elevated pericardial pressure prevents early RV filling) | Constrictive: prominent X and Y descents (pericardial constraint at end-diastole only) | ECHO → pericardial fluid + RV collapse (tamponade) | Diastolic equalisation → seen in BOTH",t:180},
  {q:"A patient presents with coarctation of the aorta. Characteristic X-ray findings include:",a:"Boot-shaped heart and oligaemic lung fields",b:"Rib notching (inferior) and figure-of-3 sign on CXR",c:"Cardiomegaly with pulmonary plethora only",d:"Mediastinal widening",ans:"B",e:"Coarctation of the aorta causes rib notching on the inferior surface of ribs 3-8 from dilated intercostal arteries (serving as collaterals via internal thoracic → anterior intercostal → posterior intercostal → descending aorta) and a figure-of-3 sign from the pre- and post-stenotic dilatation on CXR.<br><br>Take Notes: Coarctation → juxtaductal (most common), near ligamentum arteriosum | Figure-of-3 sign → indentation of aorta + pre/post-stenotic dilatation | Rib notching → ribs 3-8 (1st and 2nd ribs not notched as superior intercostals from subclavian are the source) | Features → upper limb hypertension + lower limb hypotension + radio-femoral delay | Associations → bicuspid aortic valve (50-80%), Turner syndrome | Treatment → balloon dilation + stenting (endovascular) or surgical repair",t:180},
  {q:"Lung cancer is staged with the TNM system. Which stage indicates surgery may provide cure in non-small cell lung cancer?",a:"Stage IV (M1a or M1b)",b:"Stage IIIB (contralateral mediastinal nodes, N3)",c:"Stage I and II (and selected Stage IIIA with resectable N2 disease)",d:"Stage IIIA with N2 disease is always contraindicated for surgery",ans:"C",e:"Surgical resection can be curative in NSCLC Stage I and II (T1-3, N0-1), and in carefully selected Stage IIIA patients (ipsilateral mediastinal nodes, N2 disease, single station, limited bulk) after neoadjuvant chemotherapy ± radiation - these patients should be discussed at MDT.<br><br>Take Notes: Stage I → lobectomy (gold standard) | Stage II → lobectomy + mediastinal lymph node dissection | Stage IIIA N2 → neoadjuvant chemo ± RT then surgery in selected cases | Stage IIIB (N3, T4N2) → definitive chemoradiation | Stage IV → systemic therapy (targetted agents for EGFR/ALK mutations) | VATS lobectomy → minimally invasive, equivalent oncological outcomes to open, faster recovery",t:180},
  {q:"A 55-year-old male undergoes aortic valve replacement (AVR) for severe aortic stenosis. Six months later he develops fever, new murmur, and positive blood cultures growing Streptococcus bovis. The most important next investigation to exclude is:",a:"Echocardiography of the prosthetic valve",b:"Colonoscopy to exclude colorectal carcinoma",c:"CT angiography of the thoracic aorta",d:"MRI brain to exclude cerebral abscess",ans:"B",e:"Streptococcus bovis (now reclassified as S. gallolyticus) bacteraemia is a sentinel for colorectal pathology - there is a well-established 25-50% association with colorectal polyps or carcinoma. Colonoscopy is mandatory in all patients with S. bovis bacteraemia even without GI symptoms.<br><br>Take Notes: S. bovis / S. gallolyticus → always colonoscopy | Also associated with colorectal cancer → Clostridium septicum bacteraemia | Prosthetic valve endocarditis → also needs ECHO (TOE preferred) | Early PVE (<60 days) → S. epidermidis, S. aureus | Late PVE (>60 days) → viridans streptococci, S. bovis | Both → IV antibiotics ± surgical valve replacement (if unstable, uncontrolled infection, abscess)",t:180},
  {q:"The most common cancer of the skin overall is:",a:"Malignant melanoma",b:"Squamous cell carcinoma",c:"Basal cell carcinoma (BCC)",d:"Merkel cell carcinoma",ans:"C",e:"BCC is the most common skin cancer worldwide (accounting for ~80% of all non-melanoma skin cancers) - it arises from basal cells of the epidermis, almost never metastasises, and is primarily a locally destructive tumour cured by excision, radiotherapy, or Mohs surgery.<br><br>Take Notes: BCC → most common cancer overall (skin) | SCC → second most common, can metastasise (especially in immunocompromised) | Melanoma → third most common skin cancer but highest mortality | Nodular BCC → most common subtype (pearly translucent nodule with telangiectasia and rolled edge) | Superficial BCC → trunk, young patients | Morphoeic BCC → most aggressive locally, poorly defined margins | Gorlin syndrome → multiple BCCs + calcified falx cerebri + jaw keratocysts",t:180},
  {q:"In reconstructive surgery, the 'angiosome' concept describes:",a:"The area of skin supplied by a single perforating vessel from the underlying artery",b:"The total skin surface area in the body",c:"The distance a flap can be safely raised from its pedicle",d:"The zone of necrosis in a random pattern flap",ans:"A",e:"An angiosome is a composite unit of tissue (skin, muscle, bone) supplied by a named source artery and its perforators - the concept, developed by Taylor and Palmer, underpins perforator flap design (e.g., DIEP flap from deep inferior epigastric perforators) and wound healing in peripheral vascular disease.<br><br>Take Notes: Angiosome → three-dimensional vascular territory supplied by one source artery | Choke vessels → anastomoses between adjacent angiosomes (can be 'recharged' to extend flap territory) | Perforator flaps → based on single perforating vessel (DIEP, SIEP, propeller flaps) | Clinical application → diabetic foot wound healing impaired when supplying angiosome artery is occluded | Taylor/Palmer → described 40 angiosomes in the body",t:180},
  {q:"A patient has severe frostbite of the toes. What is the most important initial management?",a:"Immediate amputation to prevent gangrene spread",b:"Rubbing with snow or warm water to re-warm quickly",c:"Rapid re-warming in a warm water bath (37-40°C) once the risk of refreezing is eliminated",d:"Leave blisters intact and rewarm slowly at room temperature",ans:"C",e:"Rapid re-warming in 37-40°C water for 15-30 minutes is the cornerstone of frostbite treatment - do NOT rewarm if there is risk of refreezing (causes more damage). Never rub with snow (causes ice crystal trauma). Blisters should be left intact (haemorrhagic blisters = worse prognosis).<br><br>Take Notes: Do NOT rewarm if risk of refreezing | Rapid rewarm → 37-40°C water bath, 15-30 minutes | Do NOT rub or massage (mechanical trauma) | Analgesia essential during rewarming (very painful) | Iloprost (prostacyclin) → for severe frostbite, reduces amputation rate | Demarcation → wait 4-6 weeks before amputation (tissue may recover better than it appears acutely) | Prevention → insulation, wet/cold avoidance",t:180},
  {q:"The MOST common indication for flap reconstruction following mastectomy is:",a:"Skin and soft tissue coverage after breast skin-sparing mastectomy",b:"Functional replacement of pectoralis major muscle",c:"Restoration of breast volume and shape following mastectomy using tissue with its own blood supply",d:"Creating nipple-areolar complex",ans:"C",e:"Autologous flap reconstruction (TRAM, DIEP, LD) recreates breast volume using the patient's own tissue after mastectomy - this provides a more natural result than implants alone, lasts a lifetime without replacement, and is particularly useful after radiotherapy when implants have high complication rates.<br><br>Take Notes: DIEP (deep inferior epigastric perforator) flap → gold standard autologous reconstruction (abdominal tissue, no muscle sacrifice) | TRAM → transverse rectus abdominis myocutaneous (muscle-based) | LD (latissimus dorsi) flap → pedicled, used alone or with implant | Timing → immediate (same operation) vs delayed reconstruction | Radiotherapy → increases implant complications (capsular contracture, infection) → favours autologous | Nipple reconstruction → tertiary procedure (tattooing or local flap)",t:180},
  {q:"Split-thickness skin grafts (STSG) differ from full-thickness skin grafts (FTSG) in that STSG:",a:"Contract more but are more likely to survive in areas with poor vascularity",b:"Have better colour and texture match and are preferred for cosmetically sensitive areas",c:"Always require primary donor site closure",d:"Cannot be used on contaminated wounds",ans:"A",e:"STSG (takes epidermis + partial dermis) contracts more due to less dermis but has a better chance of 'take' even in poorly vascularised beds - the thinner graft requires less in-growth from below. FTSG (full epidermis + complete dermis) gives better cosmesis and less contraction but requires better vascularity and a separate donor site closure.<br><br>Take Notes: STSG → more contraction + better take + donor site heals spontaneously (re-epithelialises from adnexal structures) | FTSG → less contraction + better cosmesis + donor site requires primary closure | STSG indications → large wounds, burns, contaminated wounds, poorly vascularised beds | FTSG indications → face, hands, joints (where contraction is problematic) | Graft take → requires immobility, contact, and vascularity (fibrin → plasmatic imbibition → inosculation → neovascularisation)",t:180},
  {q:"Necrotising enterocolitis (NEC) is the most common gastrointestinal emergency in preterm neonates. Bell staging is used for severity. Stage III NEC requires:",a:"NPO + antibiotics + close observation",b:"NPO + IV antibiotics + TPN + peritoneal drain only",c:"Emergency laparotomy for bowel resection (perforated NEC) or peritoneal drain as bridge",d:"Conservative management with feeds",ans:"C",e:"Bell Stage III NEC (advanced, with signs of peritonitis, perforation, or clinical deterioration despite medical management) requires surgical intervention - either primary peritoneal drainage as a bridge in extremely low birth weight infants or emergency laparotomy with bowel resection and stoma formation.<br><br>Take Notes: Bell Stage I → suspected NEC (mild symptoms, treat medically) | Stage II → definite NEC (radiological pneumatosis intestinalis, treat medically with NPO+IV ABx+TPN) | Stage III → advanced NEC (perforation or deterioration → surgery) | Pneumatosis intestinalis → intramural gas (pathognomonic of NEC on AXR) | Portal venous gas → poor prognostic sign | Complication → short bowel syndrome from extensive resection",t:180},
  {q:"A 2-day-old neonate has bilious vomiting and abdominal distension. AXR shows the 'double bubble' sign with no gas beyond the duodenum. The most appropriate operative intervention is:",a:"Ramstedt's pyloromyotomy",b:"Ladd's procedure",c:"Duodenoduodenostomy (bypass procedure)",d:"Duodenoplasty",ans:"C",e:"Duodenal atresia (double bubble = dilated stomach + dilated duodenum proximal to atresia with no distal gas) is treated by duodenoduodenostomy - a diamond-shaped anastomosis bypassing the atretic segment. Pre-operative correction of electrolyte imbalances is essential.<br><br>Take Notes: Duodenal atresia → down syndrome 30%, VACTERL associations | Double bubble sign → dilated stomach + proximal duodenum, NO gas beyond | Duodenoduodenostomy → diamond-shaped anastomosis (creates larger anastomosis than end-to-end) | Ladd's procedure → for malrotation with Ladd's bands | Delayed diagnosis → electrolyte imbalance (alkalosis from vomiting, same as pyloric stenosis if bile-free) | Bilious vomiting = bile stained = implies obstruction BELOW ampulla of Vater",t:180},
  {q:"Which of the following is a contraindication to laparoscopic pyloromyotomy (Ramstedt's) in a 4-week-old with hypertrophic pyloric stenosis?",a:"Weight <3 kg",b:"Uncorrected metabolic alkalosis and hypokalaemia",c:"Age <2 weeks",d:"Absence of a visible pyloric 'olive' on examination",ans:"B",e:"Pyloric stenosis surgery must be delayed until the metabolic alkalosis (from loss of HCl in vomit) and hypokalaemia are corrected - operating with hypokalaemia risks cardiac arrhythmia, and alkalosis causes post-anaesthetic respiratory depression. Correction typically takes 24-48 hours of IV fluids.<br><br>Take Notes: Correction → 0.45% NaCl + 20 mmol/L KCl IV | Target → urine Cl >20 mEq/L + serum Cl ≥100 + K+ normal | Surgery → NOT an emergency (unlike NEC, intussusception) | Laparoscopic vs open → equivalent outcomes, laparoscopic faster recovery | Complication → incomplete myotomy (persistent symptoms) or mucosal perforation (repair immediately) | Recurrence → very rare after complete myotomy",t:180},
  {q:"Biliary atresia, if untreated, uniformly leads to death within the first few years of life. The Kasai portoenterostomy (hepatic portoenterostomy) works by:",a:"Replacing the obliterated biliary system with a synthetic conduit",b:"Creating a direct anastomosis between a Roux-en-Y limb of jejunum and the porta hepatis, allowing bile flow via microscopic residual biliary channels",c:"Excising the liver and transplanting",d:"Reconstructing the biliary tree using the gallbladder as a conduit",ans:"B",e:"The Kasai procedure excises the fibrotic extrahepatic biliary remnant and brings a Roux-en-Y jejunal limb up to the porta hepatis - microscopic residual biliary channels at the porta hepatis drain bile into the jejunum if surgery is performed before 60 days of age.<br><br>Take Notes: Kasai before 60 days → best outcomes (50-60% achieve adequate bile flow) | After 90 days → very poor outcomes (virtually all need transplant) | Type III (no bile ducts visible) → most common type of biliary atresia | Complications → ascending cholangitis (most common early complication post-Kasai) | Long-term → 70-80% ultimately need liver transplant by adulthood | Biliary atresia → most common indication for paediatric liver transplantation",t:180},
  {q:"A 6-week-old female infant is found to have a 2 cm mass in the left side of her neck after her parents noticed it while bathing her. USS shows a unilocular thin-walled cyst in the posterior triangle. No skin changes. The most likely diagnosis is:",a:"Branchial cyst",b:"Lymphangioma (cystic hygroma)",c:"Thyroglossal duct cyst",d:"Haemangioma",ans:"B",e:"A soft, transilluminant cystic mass in the posterior triangle of a newborn/infant is a lymphangioma (cystic hygroma) - it arises from lymphatic sequestrations that fail to join the venous system. It is multilocular and can trans-illuminate brilliantly.<br><br>Take Notes: Cystic hygroma → posterior triangle of neck, present at birth or early infancy, transilluminant, soft | Large → can cause airway compromise (prenatal diagnosis = EXIT procedure) | Treatment → surgical excision or sclerotherapy (bleomycin, OK-432) | Branchial cyst → older child/adult, anterior triangle, second branchial cleft most common, pre-SCM | Thyroglossal cyst → midline, moves on tongue protrusion, at or above hyoid | Dermoid cyst → midline, floor of mouth or suprasternal",t:180},
  {q:"A 4-month-old girl has been noted by her parents to have a rapidly growing bright red lesion on her cheek that appeared at 2 weeks of age and is now 3 cm. It is soft and compressible. No other lesions. The parents are worried about cancer. The most appropriate management is:",a:"Urgent surgical excision under general anaesthesia",b:"Urgent referral for propranolol treatment",c:"Reassurance - 90% of infantile haemangiomas involute spontaneously by age 5-9",d:"Laser ablation at this stage",ans:"C",e:"Infantile haemangioma is the most common soft tissue tumour of infancy - it is absent at birth, appears at 1-4 weeks, grows rapidly to peak size at 9-12 months, then slowly involutes over years. Unless it is threatening vision, airway, or causing ulceration, reassurance and observation is the initial approach.<br><br>Take Notes: Complications requiring propranolol → visual obstruction (amblyopia risk) + airway involvement + large/ulcerating lesions | Propranolol → oral, 2 mg/kg/day, most effective when started <6 months | KASABACH-MERRITT → consumptive coagulopathy from giant haemangioma (requires treatment) | Association → PHACE syndrome (Posterior fossa, large Haemangioma of face, Arterial anomalies, Cardiac defects, Eye anomalies) | Involute → 50% by age 5, 70% by age 7, 90% by age 9",t:180},
  {q:"Anorectal malformations in neonates - which finding indicates a HIGH (supralevator) lesion requiring posterior sagittal anorectoplasty (PSARP) rather than a simple perineal procedure?",a:"Perineal fistula (meconium visible on perineum)",b:"Anal membrane (visible dimple with bulging membrane)",c:"Rectourethral fistula or rectovestibular fistula in females (no visible perineal opening, meconium in urine)",d:"Bucket-handle deformity (prominent median raphé)",ans:"C",e:"High anorectal malformations (supralevator) include rectovesical fistula in males and rectovestibular fistula in females - meconium in urine (through the urethra) indicates a rectourethral fistula, requiring colostomy at birth followed by definitive PSARP at 3-6 months when bowel decompressed.<br><br>Take Notes: Low lesion → anal membrane, perineal fistula, bucket-handle → primary perineal operation possible | High lesion → no perineal opening + meconium in urine/vagina → colostomy first + PSARP later | Sacral ratio → low (<0.4) → poor prognosis for continence | Associated anomalies → VACTERL (always screen: Echo, renal USS, spinal MRI, sacral X-ray) | PSARP (Peña) → posterior sagittal approach, excellent results for low lesions",t:180},
  {q:"Oesophageal atresia without tracheo-oesophageal fistula (Type A, 'pure' OA) differs from the common Type C (OA + distal TEF) because:",a:"It has better prognosis",b:"The abdomen is gasless on AXR (no air can reach stomach without TEF) and the gap is usually longer requiring delayed primary repair or staged reconstruction",c:"It presents later in life",d:"There is no polyhydramnios",ans:"B",e:"Type A (pure OA, no fistula) represents ~7% of OA - the gasless abdomen (no air to stomach via fistula) on AXR is the distinguishing radiological feature. The gap between upper and lower oesophageal pouches is usually long (long-gap OA), requiring delayed primary repair (gap narrows as baby grows) or staged reconstruction using stomach or colon.<br><br>Take Notes: Type C (OA + distal TEF) → most common (85%), gas in stomach (air via fistula) | Type A (pure OA) → gasless abdomen, long gap, delayed repair | Type E (H-type TEF without OA) → present later with recurrent aspiration/pneumonia | Prenatal diagnosis → polyhydramnios (cannot swallow amniotic fluid) + absent stomach bubble (Type C has bubble from TEF) | Post-repair complications → oesophageal dysmotility, reflux, stricture",t:180},
  {q:"A 7-year-old boy presents with a 6-month history of intermittent bone pain in the right tibia, initially worse at night. AXR shows a diaphyseal lesion with aggressive periosteal reaction in an 'onion skin' pattern and a large soft tissue mass. Biopsy shows small round blue cells staining positive for CD99. The diagnosis is:",a:"Osteosarcoma",b:"Ewing's sarcoma",c:"Osteomyelitis",d:"Langerhans cell histiocytosis",ans:"B",e:"Ewing's sarcoma = small round blue cells + CD99+ + diaphyseal location + onion-skin periosteal reaction in a child. The characteristic molecular abnormality is t(11;22) creating EWSR1-FLI1 fusion. Treatment is multiagent chemotherapy + surgery/radiotherapy.<br><br>Take Notes: Ewing's sarcoma → t(11;22), EWSR1-FLI1 | Age → 10-20 years, male > female | Location → diaphysis of long bones + flat bones (pelvis in older children) | Histology → small round blue cells + CD99+ (MIC-2 protein) | Osteosarcoma → osteoid production, metaphyseal, sunburst/Codman's triangle | Treatment → VAC/IE (vincristine, actinomycin, cyclophosphamide/ifosfamide) + surgery | Ewing's → 5-year survival 65% (localised)",t:180},
  {q:"An infant born at 32 weeks gestation develops respiratory distress within 4 hours of birth with grunting, chest retractions, and progressive hypoxia. CXR shows bilateral diffuse fine granular 'ground glass' opacity with air bronchograms. The diagnosis is:",a:"Meconium aspiration syndrome",b:"Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome (NRDS) from surfactant deficiency",c:"Group B streptococcal pneumonia",d:"Transient tachypnoea of the newborn",ans:"B",e:"NRDS (hyaline membrane disease) is caused by surfactant deficiency in premature infants - without surfactant, alveoli collapse at end-expiration (Laplace law), causing progressive atelectasis, hypoxia, and 'ground glass' appearance on CXR with air bronchograms.<br><br>Take Notes: NRDS → premature infants (<35 weeks), surfactant deficiency, type II pneumocytes immature | Treatment → CPAP + surfactant replacement (intratracheal) + supplemental O2 | Prevention → antenatal corticosteroids (betamethasone) → accelerate lung maturity | L/S ratio → >2:1 in amniotic fluid = fetal lung maturity | Complications → chronic lung disease (bronchopulmonary dysplasia), ROP, IVH | Meconium aspiration → post-term, meconium staining, normal/larger infant",t:180},
  {q:"A 14-year-old male presents with an anterior mediastinal mass on CXR. Serum AFP is 1200 ng/mL and beta-hCG is elevated. CT shows a 8 cm heterogeneous mass compressing the superior vena cava. The most likely diagnosis is:",a:"Hodgkin lymphoma (most common anterior mediastinal mass in adolescents)",b:"Non-seminomatous germ cell tumour (NSGCT) of the mediastinum",c:"Thymoma",d:"Metastatic Wilms tumour",ans:"B",e:"Primary mediastinal NSGCT presents in young males with an anterior mediastinal mass and elevated AFP and/or beta-hCG - the elevated AFP is pathognomonic of non-seminomatous GCT (yolk sac tumour component). Pure seminoma does not elevate AFP. Treatment is BEP chemotherapy followed by resection of residual mass.<br><br>Take Notes: Anterior mediastinum masses (4 Ts) → Thymoma, Teratoma/GCT, Terrible lymphoma, Thyroid | AFP elevation → yolk sac tumour (never in pure seminoma or pure germinoma) | Beta-hCG → choriocarcinoma component | SVC syndrome → from compression (facial/arm oedema, JVD) | BEP chemotherapy → bleomycin + etoposide + cisplatin | Mediastinal NSGCT → poorer prognosis than gonadal GCT",t:180},
  {q:"A 55-year-old woman with a 20-year history of thyroid medication use (thyroxine 100 mcg/day) presents with progressive dyspnoea and difficulty swallowing. CT shows a large anterior neck mass with substernal extension and tracheal deviation >1 cm. Thyroid function tests are normal. The most appropriate next investigation and plan is:",a:"Trial of increased thyroxine dose for 3 months to suppress TSH",b:"Radioiodine therapy as first-line",c:"Fine needle aspiration cytology + urgent thyroid ultrasound + surgical planning for total thyroidectomy with sternotomy access",d:"Observation with 3-monthly USS",ans:"C",e:"A large substernal goitre causing tracheal deviation >1 cm with dysphagia and dyspnoea is a surgical emergency risk - FNAC evaluates malignancy, and surgical planning should include preparation for possible sternotomy access (when 50% or more of the gland is substernal) to obtain mediastinal vascular control.<br><br>Take Notes: Substernal goitre → >50% gland in thorax | Indications for surgery → symptoms (dysphagia, dyspnoea, stridor, SVC syndrome) | Surgical access → cervical + sternotomy if needed for vascular control | Radioiodine → reduces size 40% but slowly; not first-line for obstructive symptoms | FNAC → always to exclude malignancy | Recurrent laryngeal nerve → often stretched by large goitre (even benign)",t:180},
  {q:"A 60-year-old male presents with haemoptysis, weight loss, and a 4 cm cavitating lesion in the right upper lobe on CT. Sputum AFB smear is positive on 3 occasions. The most important initial management is:",a:"Urgent CT-guided biopsy for tissue diagnosis",b:"Immediate bronchoscopy",c:"Isolation of the patient and initiation of 4-drug anti-tuberculosis therapy (RIPE) under DOTS",d:"Thoracic surgery consultation for right upper lobectomy",ans:"C",e:"Smear-positive pulmonary TB (3 positive AFBs) is a diagnosis - immediate isolation (airborne precautions) and notification of public health, followed by DOTS-supervised 4-drug ATT (Rifampicin, Isoniazid, Pyrazinamide, Ethambutol) for 2 months then RI for 4 months, is the standard of care.<br><br>Take Notes: Smear-positive → 3 AFB positive = definitive diagnosis | DOTS → directly observed treatment short-course (improves adherence) | Notification → mandatory (public health measure) | Isolation → until smear-negative (typically 2 weeks of treatment) | Culture + sensitivity → always send for drug resistance testing | MDR-TB → resistance to INH + rifampicin → 18-24 months of second-line drugs",t:180},
  {q:"A 22-year-old male presents with a 3-month history of progressively enlarged, non-tender left testicular swelling. He thought it was a hydrocele. USS shows a solid 4 cm intratesticular heterogeneous mass. AFP is 55 ng/mL, beta-hCG is 0, LDH is mildly elevated. CT shows no retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy. The next step is:",a:"Transcrotal orchidectomy",b:"Observation with repeat USS in 6 weeks",c:"Radical (inguinal) orchidectomy + retroperitoneal staging CT",d:"Trans-scrotal biopsy for histological confirmation before orchidectomy",ans:"C",e:"Any solid intratesticular mass in a young male must be treated as a testicular cancer until proven otherwise - radical orchidectomy via inguinal approach (NOT transcrotal biopsy or scrotal orchidectomy, as this would contaminate inguinal lymph nodes) provides diagnosis and staging. AFP 55 suggests non-seminomatous element.<br><br>Take Notes: Approach → ALWAYS inguinal orchidectomy (transcrotal risks inguinal node metastasis, alters drainage pattern) | Staging → CT chest/abdomen/pelvis + tumour markers | AFP elevated → NSGCT (yolk sac tumour component) | AFP normal + beta-hCG normal → pure seminoma | Stage I NSGCT → surveillance vs adjuvant BEP chemotherapy | Stage I seminoma → surveillance vs adjuvant carboplatin or RT",t:180},
  {q:"Anterior resection syndrome (low anterior resection syndrome, LARS) refers to a constellation of bowel dysfunction after low anterior resection for rectal cancer, including urgency, frequency, incomplete evacuation, and clustering. The main pathophysiology is:",a:"Disruption of the inferior hypogastric nerve plexus causing autonomic dysfunction",b:"Loss of rectal reservoir function and disruption of the rectoanal inhibitory reflex",c:"Ischaemia of the colon remnant from IMA ligation",d:"Obstruction from anastomotic stricture formation",ans:"B",e:"LARS results primarily from loss of the rectal reservoir (the rectum stores stool, allowing controlled evacuation) and disruption of the rectoanal inhibitory reflex (RAIR - internal anal sphincter relaxes when distended rectum approaches it) - without the rectum, the neorectum poorly distinguishes gas from liquid/solid and has impaired accommodation.<br><br>Take Notes: LARS score → validated tool (major LARS > minor LARS) | Risk factors → low anastomosis (<6 cm from anal verge), preoperative radiotherapy, poor sphincter function, older age | Management → dietary modification + loperamide + biofeedback + TENS + transanal irrigation | Radiotherapy → worsens LARS (fibrosis, reduced compliance) | Long-term → 50-70% patients have major LARS after ultralow resection | Definitive → rare cases benefit from colostomy",t:180},
  {q:"A 45-year-old male construction worker presents with a 2-week history of progressive dysphagia to solids and recent weight loss. Endoscopy shows a mass at 30 cm from the incisors. Biopsy shows squamous cell carcinoma. CT staging shows T3N1M0. The most appropriate treatment strategy is:",a:"Palliative stenting only",b:"Upfront oesophagectomy without neoadjuvant treatment",c:"Neoadjuvant chemoradiotherapy (CROSS protocol: carboplatin + paclitaxel + RT) followed by reassessment and surgery",d:"Definitive chemoradiotherapy without surgery",ans:"C",e:"The CROSS trial (Van Hagen 2012) demonstrated that neoadjuvant weekly carboplatin + paclitaxel + concurrent radiation (41.4 Gy) followed by oesophagectomy significantly improved median survival (49 vs 24 months) and complete pathological response rates (~30% SCC) in T2-4N0-1M0 oesophageal cancer.<br><br>Take Notes: CROSS protocol → standard for resectable oesophageal cancer (SCC or adenocarcinoma) | T3N1M0 → locally advanced but potentially resectable → neoadjuvant + surgery | Adenocarcinoma → alternative is perioperative FLOT chemotherapy (FLOT4 trial) | Definitive chemoRT → for SCC if patient refuses/unfit surgery (similar outcomes in some series) | Complete response to neoadjuvant → some advocate non-operative management | Stenting → palliative for unresectable disease",t:180},
  {q:"Triage in mass casualty incidents (MCI) uses the START triage system. A patient is tagged RED (immediate) if they have which finding?",a:"No pulse and not breathing after repositioning the airway",b:"Respiratory rate <10 or >29, absent radial pulse, or GCS <2 (fails to follow commands)",c:"Ambulatory and able to follow simple commands",d:"Minor wounds with no breathing compromise",ans:"B",e:"START (Simple Triage and Rapid Treatment) triage: RED (immediate) = respirations ≥30 or <10 (or absent but airway opening restored them), OR absent radial pulse (perfusion), OR fails to follow simple commands (mental status). These patients need immediate intervention to survive.<br><br>Take Notes: START triage → 30-2-CAN DO mnemonic | Respirations >30 → RED | No radial pulse → RED | Cannot follow commands → RED | GREEN → walking wounded (minor) | YELLOW → delayed (ambulatory or respirations 10-29 + pulse present + follows commands) | BLACK → expectant (no respirations even after airway opened, or unsurvivable) | Goal → triage all patients in <60 seconds each",t:180},
  {q:"A 30-year-old female 28 weeks pregnant presents with acute appendicitis. Which statement about appendicitis in pregnancy is MOST accurate?",a:"Laparoscopy is absolutely contraindicated in pregnancy",b:"Conservative management with antibiotics is preferred to avoid surgery",c:"Surgery should not be delayed regardless of gestational age, as perforation risk increases with delay",d:"MRI is contraindicated and CT should be used first-line",ans:"C",e:"Appendicitis in pregnancy carries significantly higher risk of perforation than non-pregnant women (due to delayed diagnosis and greater omentum migration away from the appendix by the gravid uterus) - surgery should not be delayed once diagnosis is confirmed, as perforation risks maternal and foetal mortality.<br><br>Take Notes: Diagnosis in pregnancy → USS (safe, first-line) → MRI if USS inconclusive (safe, preferred over CT) | Laparoscopy → safe in all trimesters (SAGES guidelines) | Risk → foetal loss 1.5% (uncomplicated) vs 36% (perforated appendicitis) | Appendix position → displaced superolaterally by gravid uterus (McBurney's point migrates upward) | Post-operative tocolysis → not routinely recommended | Timing → general anaesthesia risks in pregnancy (always involve obstetric team)",t:180},
  {q:"A 68-year-old male on long-term aspirin and clopidogrel for a coronary stent (placed 6 months ago) requires emergency sigmoid colectomy for perforated diverticulitis. How should antiplatelet management be handled?",a:"Discontinue both drugs immediately and proceed with surgery",b:"Reverse platelets with platelet transfusion and proceed urgently; accept the ongoing thrombotic risk of stopping dual antiplatelet therapy temporarily",c:"Delay surgery until dual antiplatelet therapy can be safely stopped (minimum 12 months post-stent)",d:"Continue both drugs and proceed with surgery accepting bleeding risk",ans:"B",e:"In emergency surgery, the bleeding must be stopped - platelet transfusion can reverse aspirin and clopidogrel effect (providing functioning platelets). Within 6 months of drug-eluting stent, stopping DAPT risks stent thrombosis (mortality 25-45%), so this risk must be discussed with cardiology and anaesthetics, and DAPT should be restarted as soon as haemostasis is achieved.<br><br>Take Notes: Drug-eluting stent → minimum 12 months DAPT (platelet activation channels recover by day 5 after stopping clopidogrel) | Emergency surgery → accept bleed risk vs thrombosis risk | Platelet transfusion → reverses DAPT effect | Stent thrombosis → catastrophic (25-45% mortality) | Aspirin → never stopped (monotherapy if one drug must continue) | Consider bridging → GP IIb/IIIa inhibitors peri-operatively in high-risk stent situations",t:180},
  {q:"The most common site of a pharyngeal pouch (Zenker's diverticulum) is:",a:"At the cricopharyngeus junction between the oropharynx and hypopharynx",b:"In the posterior wall of the hypopharynx at Killian's dehiscence (between the inferior pharyngeal constrictor and cricopharyngeus muscle)",c:"In the anterior wall of the oesophagus below the cricopharyngeus",d:"At the level of the thoracic inlet",ans:"B",e:"Killian's dehiscence is an area of relative muscular weakness between the oblique thyropharyngeal fibres and the horizontal cricopharyngeus muscle - pulsion diverticula (Zenker's) herniate posteriorly through this gap, pushed by raised intraluminal pressure against an incompletely relaxing cricopharyngeus.<br><br>Take Notes: Zenker's → false diverticulum (mucosa + submucosa only) | Presents → regurgitation of undigested food, halitosis, neck swelling (compressible) | CXR → air-fluid level in posterior mediastinum | Diagnosis → barium swallow | Treatment → endoscopic stapler division of cricopharyngeus (dohlman) or open surgical excision | Aspiration pneumonia → risk from stagnant pouch contents | Never endoscope blindly → risk of perforation into pouch",t:180},
  {q:"The most common cause of obstructive jaundice in a 70-year-old male without gallstones on USS, with a 3 cm periampullary mass on CT and a markedly raised CA 19-9, is:",a:"Cholangiocarcinoma",b:"Ampullary carcinoma",c:"Carcinoma of the head of the pancreas",d:"Duodenal carcinoma",ans:"C",e:"Pancreatic head carcinoma is the most common cause of periampullary malignancy (>85% of periampullary cancers), presenting with progressive painless obstructive jaundice, marked CA 19-9 elevation, and a solid hypoenhancing mass on CT. Whipple procedure is the only curative option if resectable.<br><br>Take Notes: Periampullary tumours → pancreatic head (most common + worst prognosis) > cholangiocarcinoma > ampullary Ca (best prognosis after resection, 40% 5yr) > duodenal Ca | CA 19-9 → not diagnostic alone, used for surveillance | Courvoisier's law → palpable non-tender gallbladder + jaundice = malignant obstruction (not gallstones) | Resectability criteria → no SMA/portal vein involvement, no extrahepatic disease | Median survival after Whipple → 18-24 months for pancreatic head Ca",t:180},
  {q:"A 45-year-old with Crohn's disease has been on infliximab for 3 years. He develops a fistula-in-ano with a complex intersphincteric track on MRI. Which statement best reflects current evidence on surgical management?",a:"Lay-open fistulotomy is appropriate for all Crohn fistulae",b:"Setons should be placed for sphincter-sparing drainage, combined with continued or optimised biological therapy (infliximab)",c:"Proctectomy is always required for perianal Crohn disease",d:"Anti-TNF therapy should be stopped before any surgical procedure",ans:"B",e:"Perianal Crohn disease is best managed by a combined surgical-medical approach: setons provide chronic drainage and prevent abscess formation while biological therapy (infliximab/adalimumab, ideally with TPMT-optimised azathioprine combination) treats the fistula track. Complete healing requires sustained remission.<br><br>Take Notes: ACCENT II trial → infliximab maintains fistula closure in 36% vs 19% placebo | Seton → non-cutting draining seton (not cutting) for Crohn perianal fistulae | Lay-open → AVOID in Crohn (high rates of incontinence and non-healing) | LIFT procedure → limited role in Crohn | Stem cell therapy (ADIPEP trial) → local adipose stem cells show promise | Proctectomy → last resort for refractory disease with rectal involvement",t:180},
  {q:"After hemicolectomy for colon cancer, the patient's 15 lymph nodes were retrieved (MINIMUM standard is 12). 4 out of 15 were positive (N2 disease). CEA is 2.3 ng/mL pre-operatively. The appropriate adjuvant treatment is:",a:"No adjuvant treatment required (surgery alone is curative)",b:"Adjuvant FOLFOX chemotherapy (oxaliplatin + 5-FU/leucovorin) for 6 months",c:"Radiotherapy alone",d:"Observation with 3-monthly CEA monitoring only",ans:"B",e:"Stage III colon cancer (N+ disease, any T) benefits from adjuvant FOLFOX chemotherapy (MOSAIC trial) - 6 months of oxaliplatin + 5-FU/leucovorin reduces recurrence risk by ~25% and improves 5-year survival from 65% to 73% in N2 disease (T and N stage-dependent).<br><br>Take Notes: FOLFOX → standard adjuvant for Stage III (N+) colon cancer | CAPOX (capecitabine + oxaliplatin) → oral equivalent option, 3 months often sufficient for low-risk Stage III | Rectal cancer → neoadjuvant chemoradiotherapy pre-operatively (not adjuvant RT) | Stage II (N0) → adjuvant chemo only for high-risk features (T4, perforation, <12 nodes, MSS microsatellite stable) | CEA → post-operative monitoring (elevation suggests recurrence) | MSI-H Stage IV → immunotherapy (pembrolizumab)",t:180},
  {q:"Which of the following correctly describes Brunner's glands and their clinical significance?",a:"They are located in the stomach and secrete pepsinogen",b:"They are located in the submucosa of the duodenum and secrete bicarbonate-rich alkaline mucus to protect against gastric acid",c:"They are found in the small intestine and produce digestive enzymes",d:"They secrete intrinsic factor in the terminal ileum",ans:"B",e:"Brunner's glands are submucosal glands unique to the duodenum (most concentrated in D1/D2) - they secrete alkaline mucus rich in bicarbonate, HCO3-, and mucin to protect the duodenal mucosa from the acidic gastric juice entering from the stomach. They are stimulated by secretin, vagal tone, and intraluminal acid.<br><br>Take Notes: Brunner's glands → submucosal, duodenum only (distinguishes duodenum histologically from other GI segments) | Brunner's gland hyperplasia → can form polypoid masses (benign, usually asymptomatic) | Stimulated by → secretin + vagal stimulation | Mucous output → protects mucosa from acid + pepsin | Mucosal glands (crypts of Lieberkühn) → throughout small intestine + colon",t:180},
  {q:"In the management of acute upper GI bleeding from peptic ulcer, the Rockall score is used post-endoscopy. A patient with a pulsatile bleeding vessel at endoscopy (Forrest Ia) is Rockall re-bleeding risk HIGH or LOW, and what endoscopic treatment is indicated?",a:"Low risk; observe only",b:"Intermediate risk; PPI infusion only",c:"High risk (Forrest Ia/Ib); combination endoscopic therapy (injection adrenaline + thermal/clip) + IV PPI infusion",d:"High risk; surgery immediately without endoscopic attempt",ans:"C",e:"Forrest Ia (spurting) and Ib (oozing) represent active arterial haemorrhage with highest re-bleeding rates (55-100% without treatment) - international guidelines recommend combination endoscopic therapy (adrenaline injection 1:10,000 + mechanical [haemoclip] or thermal [heater probe]) plus high-dose IV PPI (80 mg bolus + 8 mg/hr infusion for 72 hours).<br><br>Take Notes: Forrest classification → Ia (active spurting) + Ib (active oozing) = HIGH risk | IIa (visible vessel) = high risk | IIb (adherent clot) = intermediate | IIc (flat pigmented spot) + III (clean base) = LOW risk | Rockall score → includes age, shock, comorbidities, endoscopic findings | Second look endoscopy → only if clinical re-bleeding | TIPS → for variceal bleeding refractory to endoscopy | H. pylori testing → all PUD patients",t:180},
  {q:"A surgeon is considering a laparoscopic versus open Nissen fundoplication for a 45-year-old with refractory GORD (confirmed by pH monitoring). The most important advantage of laparoscopic over open Nissen fundoplication is:",a:"Better long-term reflux control",b:"Significantly lower morbidity, shorter hospital stay, and faster return to work with equivalent 10-year outcomes",c:"Lower risk of post-operative dysphagia",d:"No risk of wrap migration into the thorax",ans:"B",e:"Laparoscopic Nissen fundoplication has equivalent long-term anti-reflux efficacy to open surgery but with significantly less post-operative pain, shorter hospital stay (1-2 vs 5-7 days), faster return to work, and lower overall complication rates - making it the gold standard approach when surgical fundoplication is indicated.<br><br>Take Notes: Nissen fundoplication → 360° wrap around lower oesophagus + fundus | Toupet → 270° posterior wrap (less dysphagia, similar reflux control) | Indications → refractory GORD confirmed on pH monitoring + normal oesophageal motility | Contraindications → poor oesophageal motility (high dysphagia risk) | Complications → dysphagia (early) + gas-bloat syndrome + slipped wrap | LINX device → magnetic sphincter augmentation (alternative minimally invasive) | Proton pump failure → consider impaired acid secretion vs non-acid reflux",t:180},
  {q:"The Forrest classification is used to assess risk of re-bleeding from peptic ulcer at endoscopy. Forrest class IIb (adherent clot) is best managed by:",a:"Vigorous clot removal followed by treatment of underlying vessel + IV PPI",b:"Discharge on oral PPI only",c:"Immediate surgery without endoscopic attempt",d:"Repeat endoscopy in 24 hours without treatment now",ans:"A",e:"Forrest IIb (adherent clot overlying vessel) has 22-35% re-bleeding risk - current evidence supports gentle clot removal with cold irrigation followed by treatment of the underlying vessel (heater probe or clips) + high-dose IV PPI infusion.<br><br>Take Notes: Forrest Ia/Ib → active bleeding, treat immediately | Forrest IIa (visible vessel) → 43% re-bleed without treatment | Forrest IIb (clot) → 22% re-bleed, debated management | Forrest IIc/III → clean base, low risk, can discharge early | NICE → IV esomeprazole 80 mg bolus + 8 mg/hr x 72h after endoscopy",t:180},
  {q:"A 50-year-old male undergoes elective repair of a 5.8 cm AAA via EVAR. Post-operatively on day 1, CT shows contrast filling the aneurysm sac with no obvious entry point from the graft - classified as which endoleak type?",a:"Type II endoleak (retrograde flow from patent collateral vessels, e.g., lumbar arteries or IMA)",b:"Type I endoleak (attachment site failure at proximal or distal graft seal zone)",c:"Type III endoleak (graft component junction failure)",d:"Type IV endoleak (graft porosity)",ans:"A",e:"Type II endoleak (most common, ~20-25% after EVAR) is caused by retrograde filling of the sac from patent side branches - IMA, lumbar arteries - without involvement of the graft seal zone. Most resolve spontaneously but persistent/enlarging sac requires intervention (embolisation of feeding vessel).<br><br>Take Notes: Type I → most serious (proximal or distal seal failure) → requires urgent reintervention | Type II → collateral backfill (IMA, lumbar) → most common, often self-limiting | Type III → modular graft separation → reintervention | Type IV → graft fabric porosity → rare with modern grafts | Endoleak surveillance → CT at 1 month, 6 months, then annually",t:180},
  {q:"Which of the following represents the correct definition of sensitivity in the context of a diagnostic test?",a:"The proportion of disease-free individuals who correctly test negative (TN/TN+FP)",b:"The proportion of all test-positive individuals who truly have disease (TP/TP+FP)",c:"The proportion of truly diseased individuals who test positive (TP/TP+FN)",d:"The probability that a negative test result rules out disease",ans:"C",e:"Sensitivity = TP/(TP+FN) - measures how well the test identifies true cases of disease. High sensitivity minimises false negatives and is used for screening (SnNout rule).<br><br>Take Notes: Sensitivity = TP/(TP+FN) | Specificity = TN/(TN+FP) | PPV = TP/(TP+FP) - prevalence dependent | NPV = TN/(TN+FN) - prevalence dependent | SnNout → high Sensitivity rules OUT | SpPin → high Specificity rules IN",t:180},
  {q:"A 25-year-old previously healthy female presents 48 hours after a road traffic accident with progressive breathlessness. ABG shows PaO2 55 mmHg on 60% O2. CXR shows bilateral diffuse infiltrates. No evidence of cardiac failure. PCWP is 10 mmHg. The diagnosis and ventilatory strategy are:",a:"ARDS; lung-protective ventilation with low tidal volumes (6 mL/kg ideal body weight) and PEEP (ARDSNet protocol)",b:"Cardiogenic pulmonary oedema; diuretics and GTN",c:"Pneumonia; broad-spectrum antibiotics and high-flow O2",d:"PE; anticoagulation and thrombolysis",ans:"A",e:"ARDS criteria (Berlin definition): acute onset + bilateral infiltrates + PaO2/FiO2 <300 + not fully explained by cardiac failure/fluid overload (PCWP <18 mmHg). Lung-protective ventilation (6 mL/kg IBW, plateau pressure <30 cmH2O) reduces ventilator-induced lung injury (ARDSNet trial, 22% mortality reduction).<br><br>Take Notes: ARDS severity → mild PF 200-300, moderate 100-200, severe <100 | Lung-protective ventilation → 6 mL/kg + PEEP + permissive hypercapnia | Prone positioning → improves oxygenation in moderate-severe ARDS (PROSEVA trial) | Steroids → dexamethasone improves outcomes in established ARDS (DEXA-ARDS trial) | ECMO → for refractory ARDS",t:180},
  {q:"The commonest organism causing acute bacterial meningitis in adults (18-50 years) in the UK is:",a:"Neisseria meningitidis",b:"Streptococcus pneumoniae",c:"Haemophilus influenzae type b",d:"Listeria monocytogenes",ans:"B",e:"Streptococcus pneumoniae (pneumococcus) is the most common cause of bacterial meningitis in adults worldwide, with high mortality (20-30%) and significant morbidity. N. meningitidis predominates in adolescents and young adults; Listeria in elderly/immunocompromised.<br><br>Take Notes: By age group → neonates: GBS + E.coli + Listeria | Children/teens: N. meningitidis | Adults: S. pneumoniae | Elderly/immunocompromised: Listeria + S. pneumoniae | Treatment → ceftriaxone + dexamethasone (before antibiotics if possible) | Dexamethasone → reduces hearing loss in pneumococcal meningitis",t:180},
  {q:"A neonate with Down syndrome presents with bilious vomiting and abdominal distension at 36 hours. AXR shows dilated loops of small bowel with air-fluid levels. A contrast enema shows a narrow 'microcolon'. The diagnosis is:",a:"Pyloric stenosis",b:"Hirschsprung disease",c:"Duodenal atresia",d:"Meconium ileus (from cystic fibrosis or ileal atresia with inspissated meconium)",ans:"D",e:"A microcolon (unused small calibre colon) on contrast enema in a neonate with biliary obstruction pattern + dilated small bowel loops indicates meconium ileus or ileal/jejunal atresia - the colon is small because it has never been used. Duodenal atresia would show double bubble without distal gas.<br><br>Take Notes: Microcolon → indicates distal small bowel obstruction (ileal atresia, meconium ileus, colonic atresia) | Meconium ileus → CF in 90%, Gastrografin enema (therapeutic + diagnostic) | Ileal atresia → apple-peel or type III | Down syndrome → associated with Hirschsprung + duodenal atresia (different radiological pattern) | Bilious vomiting → obstruction distal to ampulla of Vater",t:180},
  {q:"The Cauda equina syndrome is a surgical emergency. Which investigation should be performed URGENTLY and which structure is compressed?",a:"CT myelogram; compression of the spinal cord at L4-L5",b:"Plain X-ray lumbar spine; compression of the cauda equina nerve roots at any level",c:"MRI lumbar spine; compression of the cauda equina nerve roots (L2-S5) below the conus medullaris",d:"Nerve conduction studies; peripheral nerve compression",ans:"C",e:"CES = compression of the cauda equina (L2-S5 nerve roots below the conus at L1-L2) causing saddle anaesthesia + bladder/bowel dysfunction + bilateral leg weakness. Urgent MRI (within 4h ideally) confirms the level and cause (disc, tumour, haematoma).<br><br>Take Notes: Conus medullaris → ends at L1-L2 in adults | Cauda equina → L2-S5 roots descend as the 'horse's tail' | CES incomplete → better prognosis, urgent decompression within 48h | CES complete → emergency decompression | Most common cause → large central L4/L5 or L5/S1 disc prolapse | Bladder dysfunction → earliest reliable symptom",t:180},
  {q:"A 67-year-old male presents with a 6-month history of progressive dysphagia to solids, regurgitation of undigested food eaten hours earlier, and halitosis. There is no weight loss. Barium swallow shows a posterior diverticulum at the pharyngo-oesophageal junction. The diagnosis is:",a:"Oesophageal carcinoma",b:"Achalasia of the cardia",c:"Plummer-Vinson syndrome",d:"Pharyngeal pouch (Zenker's diverticulum)",ans:"D",e:"Zenker's diverticulum classically presents with regurgitation of undigested food (eaten hours earlier, no bile), halitosis, gurgling neck sounds, and progressive dysphagia in elderly males. The posterior herniation through Killian's dehiscence stores food and debris between meals.<br><br>Take Notes: Killian's dehiscence → between thyropharyngeus and cricopharyngeus muscle | Barium swallow → posterior diverticulum at pharyngo-oesophageal junction | Treatment → endoscopic stapler division (dohlman procedure) or open surgery | Risk → aspiration pneumonia from stagnant pouch contents | Never endoscope blindly → risk of pouch perforation | Distinguished from achalasia → barium shows dilated oesophagus with bird's beak in achalasia",t:180},
  {q:"In pancreatic head carcinoma requiring a Whipple procedure (pancreaticoduodenectomy), which anastomosis is associated with the highest risk of post-operative leak and mortality?",a:"Choledochojejunostomy (bile duct to jejunum)",b:"Gastrojejunostomy (stomach to jejunum)",c:"Pancreaticojejunostomy (pancreatic duct to jejunum)",d:"Hepaticojejunostomy (hepatic duct to jejunum)",ans:"C",e:"Pancreatic fistula/leak from the pancreaticojejunostomy is the most feared and most common serious complication after Whipple - occurring in 15-25% of cases. The soft pancreatic texture + small duct (<3mm) + fatty parenchyma are the highest risk factors for leak.<br><br>Take Notes: Pancreatic fistula → ISGPF Grade A (biochemical), B (clinical), C (life-threatening) | Risk factors → soft pancreas, small duct, high BMI, blood loss | Prophylaxis → octreotide (debated), fibrin glue, stent across anastomosis | Management → IR-guided drain, re-operation for Grade C | Delayed gastric emptying → most common complication overall | Operative mortality → <5% at high-volume centres",t:180}
];
